# Flow API design

This document is the design of flows and the steps to reach it: the properties every part of the language respects, the design of flows and of each operation, examples with the imperative code each strategy generates, the combinations of operators, the ways an implementation can look correct and be wrong, and the implementation stages. The notes at the end are guidance for whoever works on this document.

General Facts:

Fearless has two kinds of exceptions: deterministic and non deterministic (like stack overflow).
They both use an imm Info object instead of an arbitrary exception object.
`Try#` catches deterministic exceptions anywhere; observing a non deterministic one needs `mut CapTry` from `mut System`.
`Repr` and `IsoPod` both offer reads and mutation in place (`look`, `mutate`); an `IsoPod` can also be opened once, giving its `iso` back and staying empty from then on.

## Vocabulary:
Use 'throw'; do not use: raise
Exception is used to talk about the still uncaught Info object.
'catch' is what Try# does; 'capture' is what a lambda does
'cancel': stop a running call from outside, through `Yield#`. Not: terminate; terminate is only used in "does not terminate".
'held': an exception not yet allowed to propagate out of the flow expression. Not: kept on hold, wait.
'discard': drop a held exception or a computed result for good. Not: never surfaces, never reported.
'garbage collectable': an object no reference leads to. Not: garbage, unreachable.
'structurally equivalent' Not: structurally identical, equal, identical, same.
'identity equal': Java ==; do not use language confusing == and structurally equivalent.
'executed as part of the xxx stage' (for example the map stage):
user code, provided via a lambda passed to that stage.
Do not use general terms like "earlier", "first", "later" etc.
'flow order': the order of the elements arriving at one operation. Not: source order, index order.
'index': the place of an element in the source, as `j` in `ej`; after a `filter`, `flatMap` or `sort` the index of an element is not its place in the flow order. 
'data parallelism/fork-join': the elements are split into chunks; a task runs one chunk through a sequence of stages; workers execute tasks.
'worker'. Not: thread, core.
'data parallel region': consecutive stages run with data parallelism. Not: parallel region, section.
'pipeline parallelism/nodes': stages run in nodes; a node receives elements in flow order and sends its output to the next node. Elements can be in chunks or not.
'node': one part of pipeline parallelism, as an actor in actor systems. Not: actor, worker; actor is only the `actor` operation, do not use 'actor' as a worker entity to avoid confusion.
'desugar/desugaring'. Not: sugar, sugaring, semantic reduction, reduce, implemented on top of, encoded on top of.
'other approaches': flow and stream libraries of other languages. Not: other libraries, other systems, similar stream APIs.
'returns': includes the result value, deterministic exceptions and non termination. Do not use 'produces' 'evaluate into'.
'completes successfully': the sub set of 'returns' including only the result value.
'mode': `Par`, `ParRepr` or `ParPod`, enabled by the type of the source. Not: execution mode, par mode.
'strategy': how the terminal runs the whole flow expression: the sequential strategy, data parallelism, pipeline parallelism, or a mix of them. Not: mode, shape, parallel shape.
'the sequential strategy': runs the elements through the stages one at a time; every other strategy must be observably equal to it. Not: sequential execution, sequential run, sequential mode.
'emits', 'pulls', 'arrives at': a stage emits an element, the next operation pulls it, and the element arrives at that operation. Not: reach, move, go past, come out, hand on, send downstream, deliver, release.
'reach': only about the ROG: an object reaches what is in its ROG.
'pass': give an argument to a call. Not: hand.
'fence'. Not: fence operation, barrier.
'positive speculation', 'negative speculation'. Not: speculative execution, speculate.
'speculative call': a call the sequential strategy may not make, or may make only after a call that throws.
'whole flow expression': a source, its stages and its terminal. Not: this flow.
'chain': what the flow object records of the whole flow expression: the source, its mode and each operation with its arguments. Not: pipeline; pipeline is only pipeline parallelism.
'shape': the chain without the values of its arguments: the sequence of the operations, the mode and the element types the operations reveal (see **Ordering errors**).
'misuse exception': the non deterministic exception a flow expression throws because of how it is written: the identity check, `onlyImmCapture`, positive speculation over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`, the ordering errors. Not: misuse error, mode error.


## Properties to be respected by any part of the language

**Determinism:**\
A method with no mut parameters (including the receiver) returns structurally equivalent results for structurally equivalent input. "Results" includes deterministic exceptions and non termination: the result may be non termination or a structurally equivalent thrown Info.

This works mostly because every method of an object capability doing something non deterministic is a mut method: with no mut in input, any capability in the ROG of the call is 'muted'. Most of the properties below also support determinism, but are valuable on their own.


**Immutability:**\
An imm reference refers to an object whose ROG is deeply unmodifiable: nothing in that ROG will ever change again.

**read:**\
A read reference can not be used to mutate an object or anything in its ROG.
If a method has no mut parameters, any read parameter is also deeply unmodifiable for the duration of the call, since no mut reference to its ROG can be in scope. This allows us to reason inductively about deterministic parallelism: if a read binding `r` is in scope, there will be no other active parallel execution with a mut reference to any part of `r`'s ROG.

**iso:**\
An iso reference refers to an object whose (mutable) ROG is encapsulated under that single reference. The ROG may share imm objects, which are referentially transparent, but every mutable object in it would be garbage collectable if that iso reference were lost.

**Strong exception safety:**\
The body of a try either completes successfully or leaves the state observable outside of the try unmodified. Enforced by typing: `Try#[R](try: read F[R])`. A `read` lambda can not mutate what it captures, so the body reaches only what it created or the `iso` given to the two-argument form, and `Action` yields the value or the `Info`.

Example: with `rs` of type `mut List[mut Repr[T]]`,
`Try#{.. rs.flow(ParRepr#).map{r -> r.mutate{...}}.list}`
compiles only if `rs` is created inside the `{..` part, not captured by the `Try` lambda.
Thus running that flow with data parallelism is correct: even if the code executed as part of the map stage throws on the element at index 3 of 7 of `rs`, no non determinism is observable, since all of ROG(rs) is garbage collectable when the exception is caught.


**Identity Transparency:**\
Without the mut methods of capability objects, we can not distinguish whether two references are identity equal or just structurally equivalent.\
This is enforced by careful API design in all the `_Magic!` setups, and is needed for Determinism and caching: otherwise one could call something twice and tell re-computation from a cached result, violating Determinism of an outer expression that internally uses caching (and tests for identity).

## Flows overall design

A flow expression is a source, then stages, then one terminal.
The terminal picks the strategy by looking at all the stages in the chain; this enables optimisations, like skipping unneeded internal materialisations.
The source and the stages only record what they are: none of them picks a strategy, or starts a task, when it is called. A rule such as "a source of three elements runs as a pipeline" taken at the source can not see the terminal, and can not know whether the chain needs positive speculation.

**Single use:**\
A flow is consumed by the call that adds the next stage or the terminal: calling a stage or a terminal on a flow that is already consumed throws. Nothing has run yet when this is detected, and whether a flow is consumed is known without looking at any element, so the exception is deterministic, thrown by that call, as for `.limit 0`. <HELP ME WITH WORDING> Example message: ``This flow was already consumed by a call of `.map`; a flow can be used once. To use the elements twice, collect them with `.list` or `.let`.``

**Lazy:** No code executed as part of a stage runs until the terminal is called.

**Flow abstraction:**\
Observably, the flow must behave as if it trickled the elements through the stages one at a time.
Fences are the only deviation: a fence emits nothing until every stage before it has finished with every element, and if its lambda can mutate what it captured (only .forEach) it does not run that lambda until then either, except as a speculative call whose exceptions are held (see **forEach**). A fence may still work as elements arrive - a sort may place each one as it arrives - provided it emits nothing, lets no exception propagate, and never keeps a stage before it from running, until every stage before it has finished with every element.
What a fence restricts is observable: with `read` and `imm` elements the stages after a fence may run before it opens, as positive speculation, provided their exceptions are held until it opens (see **example with a fence**).

It must not be observable whether a flow runs the stages one at a time or passes the elements one at a time through all the stages, whether it does internal materialisations, etc.
A stronger property, that how the lambdas are run is not observable at all, is out of reach: when two calls throw, the exception that propagates is the one at the smallest index, so the order of the calls is observable through it. The property above is worded to keep that window as small as it is.

**Flow unobservable parallelism:**\
It must not be observable whether a potentially parallel flow actually runs in parallel or sequentially. This is intentionally slightly stronger than what Determinism alone needs.
'Observable' here means observable without capabilities. Through `mut CapTry` from `mut System` a program can observe non determinism, including which strategy ran a flow: for example, catching the exception of a flow over `Repr`s with a `CapTry` whose body captures the `Repr`s shows which elements were mutated under negative speculation. This is fine: Determinism only covers methods with no mut parameters, and using `CapTry` needs `mut System`.

Where a method contract is purposely under-specified, such as the tournament order in max, min and sort, the same choice must be made in every strategy: a different winner under a parallel strategy and the sequential strategy would make the strategy observable.

This property enables a crucial optimisation: a terminal can check at its start whether all the workers are busy (`allTheCoresBusy`) and, if so, run that flow sequentially.

**Semantics:**\
The semantics is given by multiple descriptions, validated by checking that they all agree:
1. the simple composition of all the stages and the terminal;
2. desugaring all flow operations into more primitive ones, until a few operations remain with an un-optimised but trivially correct direct implementation;
3. examples of many thorny cases;
4. translation of full flow expressions into traditional imperative statements.

### Stages and terminals

**Stages** are the intermediate operations returning another flow.

**Elementwise:** `map`, `filter`, `flatMap`, `flatMapMut`, and `peek` and `mapFilter`, which desugar to them. The output for an element depends only on that element, so any element can run on any worker.

**Streaming stateful:** `limit`, `scan`, `actor`, `actorMut`, `map with context`, `distinct`. They need the elements in order and emit as they go.

**Fence:** `sort`, `sortDistinct`, `max`, `min`. They consume everything before emitting anything.

**Source combinator:** `++`. A fence that then appends the elements of a list to those of the flow, rather than acting on elements.

**Terminals** end the flow and return something that is not a flow.

**Consuming:** `list`, `eList`, `set`, `eSet`, `mapping`, `forEach`, `fold`, `foldAcc`, `size`, `isEmpty`, `get`, `getOpt`, `join`, `sum`, `last`, `let`. Every element passes the whole chain.

**Short-circuit:** `first`, `any`, `all`, `none`, `findFirst`, `findMap`. They can decide before the end, leaving the elements after the decision in flow order never pulled.

### Speculation

**Positive speculation** runs stages on elements that the sequential strategy never pulls, as when a short-circuit terminal checks 8 elements in parallel and returns the one with the smallest index that satisfies its predicate. Over `Repr`s this is unsound: it can mutate elements that the sequential strategy would not mutate, and the mutation stays visible after the result.

**Negative speculation** runs stages on elements after, in flow order, one whose deterministic exception stops the sequential strategy: even `list.flow.map f .list` must throw the exception of the smallest index whose map call throws, whatever the other tasks throw. Strong exception safety makes the work done on greater indexes garbage collectable once the exception is caught.

In every parallel strategy below, an exception thrown by a call is held until two things are known: whether the sequential strategy makes that call at all, and whether every call the sequential strategy makes before it completes successfully. The exception propagates only if the sequential strategy makes that call and every call it makes before that call completes successfully. Otherwise the exception is discarded: under positive speculation when the sequential strategy never makes that call, under negative speculation when a call it makes before that call throws. If a call the sequential strategy makes before that call does not terminate, the exception is held forever. Speculative work that can no longer affect the result can be cancelled only if it uses `Yield#` correctly.

**Termination:**\
The whole flow expression returns as soon as the sequential strategy would return: no terminal waits for a call the sequential strategy does not make, or makes after a call that throws. A speculative call that does not terminate and never reaches a `Yield#` keeps its worker for the life of the process, but never delays the result. Two designs break this without looking wrong:
- joining every task before the terminal returns: a speculative call that does not terminate then makes the whole flow expression not terminate where the sequential strategy returns;
- reading the output of a task only once the whole task has finished: the terminal waits for the elements of the task after its decision, and one of them may not terminate. The output of a task arrives at the terminal element by element, as the mailboxes of **Pseudocode** send it.

**Non deterministic exceptions of speculative calls:**\
They are discarded exactly as the deterministic ones: a stack overflow or an out of memory error thrown by a call the sequential strategy does not make never propagates, and one thrown at a greater index than a deterministic exception never replaces it. Letting them propagate would not break Determinism, since observing them needs `mut CapTry`, but a program would crash under a parallel strategy where it completes successfully under the sequential strategy. The exceptions that must propagate whatever the elements do are the misuse exceptions (identity check, `onlyImmCapture`, positive speculation over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`, ordering errors): they depend only on the flow expression and on the source, so they are thrown before the result of any element is used.
A worker may have a smaller stack than the calling worker, so a call may throw a stack overflow on one and complete successfully on the other: this is non determinism, allowed, but a cache of results (such as a memo) must never store a non deterministic exception, or every call through that cache from then on throws it.

**Cancellation: `Yield#`:**\
`Yield: Sealed { #: Void }`. `Yield#` is a cancellation point: in a speculative call whose result can no longer affect what the whole flow expression returns, it stops that call, and nothing the call computed is used; in every other call, under every strategy, it returns `Void` and does nothing. So it is not observable, and it can sit in any loop or recursion that may run for long, in the lambdas of a flow or in the methods they call. A speculative call that does not terminate releases its worker only through `Yield#`. It does nothing inside the body of `Repr.mutate` and `IsoPod.mutate` (see **Implementation pitfalls**).

**Positive speculation over `ParRepr` and `ParPod` is not allowed, whatever is done around it:**\
- Joining the tasks before the terminal returns does not help: the mutations of elements the sequential strategy never pulls are still there after the result, and how many elements are mutated depends on chunk sizes and scheduling. It also breaks **Termination**, since `Yield#` can not cancel a call in the middle of `mutate` (see **Implementation pitfalls**).
- Making each mutation complete, rather than stopping it halfway, only prevents a broken invariant; it does not undo the mutation.
- It is not "the price of the mode": the mode promises that the parallel execution is not observable. When a flow expression over `ParRepr` or `ParPod` could run in parallel only with positive speculation, it throws the non deterministic misuse exception of **example with filter, over ParRepr**, in every strategy, the sequential strategy included.

**Negative speculation over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`** does not have this problem when the exception is caught by `Try#`, since every element is garbage collectable. Under `mut CapTry` the caller can still reach the elements while a task is inside `mutate` on one of them, and by **Termination** the flow can not wait for that task. Open decision: a `Repr` or `IsoPod` an unfinished task is inside is marked busy, and any other use of it throws a non deterministic exception until the task leaves; this costs a store per `mutate` of an element of a flow over `ParRepr` or `ParPod`, and prevents two workers from ever running a `mut` method of one object at the same time.

### Modes

The static type of the source collection enables one or more modes. They crystallise what we know and can assume, and allow or block strategies for the terminal, including parallelisation and omission of intermediate materialisations.

**`Par[T:imm,read]`:** can be enabled if source elements are `imm` or `read`.

**`ParRepr[T]`:** can be enabled if source elements are `mut Repr[T]`. `Reprs#` takes an `iso T`, so two distinct `Repr`s share no inner state. This does not ensure the `Repr`s in the source are distinct objects: the flow runs an identity check over the source and throws a non deterministic exception when two elements are identity equal; a deterministic one would expose object identities (see **Identity Transparency**).

**`ParPod[T]`:** can be enabled if source elements are `mut IsoPod[T]`, with the same guarantee through `IsoPods#` and the same identity check.

The modes are not about the type of the elements between the stages. A stage of a flow in mode `Par` may create mutable objects, and another stage may mutate them: over an `imm List[imm Nat]`, `l.flow.map{i -> Persons#i}.map{p -> ...}` runs in parallel and every (mut) `Person` it builds is reachable from nowhere else, so the stages after it can mutate it safely.

**Duplicated `mut` elements:**\
The paragraph above stops holding after a `flatMapMut` or an `actorMut`: they can emit one `mut` object as two elements, or as parts of two elements, like two pairs holding the same `Person`.
```
numbers.flow.map{n -> Persons#n}.flatMapMut{p -> Lists#(p, p)}.filter{..}.list
```
The lambda of `filter` is a `read F`: it can not mutate what it captured, but it can mutate its `mut` parameter, so two tasks running `filter` on the two copies would race on one `Person`.
No identity check can find the shared parts, so the elements a `flatMapMut` or an `actorMut` emits from the same input can not be given to different tasks, by any split into chunks, including one after a fence, with the only exception of `++`.
`++` is a special fence: it emits every element as `read` or `imm`, so no stage after it can mutate an element, and elements sharing `mut` objects can go to different tasks (see **++**).

**`onlyImmCapture` flag:**\
The backend marks each lambda object that captures only `imm` values; the flag is visible to the runtime (in Java), not in Fearless. A top level declaration used as a value captures nothing, so it carries the flag, and so does every `imm` lambda, which can capture only `imm` values.
The identity check only compares the elements with each other, while a `read` lambda can capture any `mut` binding as `read`: the source list itself, or a local or parameter `p: mut Repr[T]` that is also one of the elements. Such a lambda can read that `Repr` while the task of its element mutates it, and the parallel execution becomes observable.
No `imm` value can reach a `mut Repr` or `mut IsoPod`, so `ParRepr` and `ParPod` need the flag on every lambda of the flow except those of `forEach` and `let`, which run once every stage has finished; without it the whole flow expression throws a non deterministic exception (see below). With the flag, each task reaches no `Repr` or `IsoPod` but its own element.

`.seqFlow` exists only on sources whose elements may be `mut`, such as `List` and `EList`, to flow them as `mut` without a mode. Where the elements are always `read` or `imm`, as in `Set` and `ESet`, `.flow` is the only entry point: running in parallel or sequentially is not observable there, so a preference for the sequential strategy is only a hint (see **Chunk sizes**).

**Sources:**\
A source is any call returning a fresh `mut Flow`; its static type decides the mode, by the rule above:
- `Par` is enabled where the type shows that every element is `read` or `imm`: `.flow` on a `read List`, an `imm List`, an `imm EList`, a `Set`, an `ESet` (which it drains), a `Map` through a `read` or `imm` reference (`.flow`, `.keys`, `.elems`), and the ranges (`0 =~~= 199`, `.flow(step)`).
- `xs.flow(mode)` on a `mut List` or a `mut EList` enables the mode passed; the type of `mode` checks the elements: `Par[T:imm,read]`, `ParRepr[T]` over `mut Repr[T]`, `ParPod[T]` over `mut IsoPod[T]`.
- No mode is enabled where the elements may be `mut` and no mode is passed: `.seqFlow` on a `mut List` or a `mut EList`, `.flow` on a `mut Map`, and `Flows#(e1, .., en)`, whose elements are `R:*`. The elements do not carry their reference capability, so a source given no mode can not enable one by looking at its elements. Only the sequential strategy runs such a flow expression, up to a `++` (see **++**).
- The flow object keeps the mode passed to `.flow(mode)`, and the checks tell the modes apart by its type, `Par`, `ParRepr` or `ParPod`.

Calling `.flow` with calls suggesting that parallelism is possible, while passing parameters or calling methods that prevent any parallelism, is most likely a user mistake. When the type system can not check this statically, a non deterministic exception is thrown. A linter could catch more cases statically, but never all of them, since flows under construction are first class values that can travel freely through the code.

The mode checks (identity check, `onlyImmCapture`, positive speculation over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`) are part of the semantics, not of the parallel strategies: every strategy runs them, the sequential strategy included, and a mode argument can never be accepted and ignored. The identity check reads the source and the `onlyImmCapture` check reads the lambdas; whether a flow expression needs positive speculation over `ParRepr` or `ParPod`, and so throws that misuse exception, is decided by its shape alone, as the ordering errors are (see **Ordering errors**): never by the size of the source, the elements, the results of the lambdas, the number passed to `limit`, the number of workers or the size of the chunks. Stating that rule exactly, operation by operation, is still to be done (see **Implementation stages**).

A good example of why a flow of Repr could be great is genetic evolution:
```
Genome: { .offspring(n: Nat): List[Genome] }
Individual: { read .genome: Genome; read .fitness: Nat; mut .evaluate: Void }

Evolve: { #(pop: mut List[mut Repr[Individual]], survivors: Nat): mut List[mut Repr[Individual]] ->
  pop.flow(ParRepr#)
    .map{r -> Block#(r.mutate{i -> i.evaluate}, r)}
    .sort(Rev#{r -> r.look{i -> i.fitness}})
    .limit survivors
    .flatMap{r -> r.look{i -> i.genome.offspring 10}}
    .map{g -> Reprs#(Individuals#g, {_ -> True})}
    .list }
```
- The `map` before the `sort` evaluates the fitness of each large individual in parallel, each task mutating only its own `Repr`, and stores the result in it. No lambda captures anything, so all carry the `onlyImmCapture` flag.
- `sort` is a fence: it waits for every fitness, and `Rev` (see **sort, sortDistinct, max, min**) emits the fittest before the others. Its `by` only reads the stored fitness, which matters since `by` computes a key on every comparison.
- `limit survivors` comes after the fence, so the count is known and there is no positive speculation. The terminal controls the sorting process, so `sort` then `limit` may run as a top `survivors` selection.
- `flatMap` turns each survivor into the `imm` genomes of its offspring; with `read`/`imm` elements the data parallel region keeps going, where `flatMapMut` would stop it (see **map, filter, flatMap**).
- The `map` before `.list` builds fresh `Repr`s in parallel (`Individuals#g` takes only an `imm` genome, so its result can be `iso`), and `.list` collects the new generation.
Note how `actor` and `actorMut` are not used in this example.

## The two kinds of parallelism

**Data parallelism/fork-join:**\
The iconic map reduce set up: `a.flow.map f1 .filter f2 .list` is divided into chunks, one task per chunk; workers execute some tasks and some are left 'for later'.
Each task conceptually applies `f1#e |> {x->f2#x .thenDo{acc.append x}}` to each element `e` of its chunk, with an accumulator `acc` that preserves the flow order.
This is often the favourite strategy.

**Pipeline parallelism/nodes:**\
What happens in actor systems: `a.flow.map f1 .filter f2 .list` is made of 3 nodes.
The `map` node receives elements from the flow (in chunks or not), applies f1 and sends them to the mailbox of the `filter` node, which applies f2 and sends the survivors to the `list` node, building the list. The 3 nodes run in parallel, and each can freely update local mutable data, since it receives its elements one at a time, in flow order.

This applies in plenty of cases where data parallelism does not, and it is more efficient when code locality is crucial: the same node runs the same code over and over, and if that code fits in cache this may beat data parallelism, especially when the whole flow expression (not broken into chunks) would not fit in cache.

## Hints

**Chunk sizes:**\
The runtime can not know how expensive a lambda is, and element count alone is a poor proxy. What are Java streams and other approaches doing? We could have some way to annotate a flow with code generation/code execution hints.
```
es.flow //examples, of course it does not make sense to use those together
  .hintSeq// to run sequential, like Java .sequential
  .hintTaskSize 10
  .hintSpeculativeSize 25
  .hintPipeline //to prefer pipeline parallelism when possible
  .hintDataParallel //to prefer fork-join parallelism when possible
  .map{..}.list
```
Then we could have unchecked exceptions on inapplicable hints etc.
A hint never changes what a flow expression returns; a hint whose misuse throws is part of the semantics (see **Stage 1: Naive**). `.suppressOrderingErrors` works like a hint (see **Ordering errors**).

## Per operation

The kinds above say what an operation is. Each section below is the design space of one operation: what an implementation is free to do with it, and what is still open. The sections state all the reasonably possible parallel strategies, not just the ones we think are the most performant.

### map, filter, flatMap

These operations act element-wise. Their lambda is a `read F[E,R]`, so it can not mutate anything it captured; the only possible mutable input is the element passed to it, which may be `mut`.

There are two flatMap signatures:
```
mut .flatMap[R:*](f: read F[E, read List[R]]): mut Flow[read/imm R]
mut .flatMapMut[R:*](f: read F[E, mut List[R]]): mut Flow[R]
```
`.flatMap` takes a `read` list and emits its elements as `read` or `imm`, as `++` does; the list of `.flatMapMut` is `mut`, since the elements of a `read` list are `read`.
The restricted signature allows more parallelism: no stage after it can mutate the elements of `.flatMap`, so it keeps a data parallel region going, while `.flatMapMut` ends it, since the elements it emits from the same input may share `mut` objects (see **Duplicated `mut` elements**).

**Desugaring:**\
map, filter and flatMap trivially desugar to flatMapMut:
```
.map f     -> this.flatMapMut{e->Lists#(f#e)}
.filter f  -> this.flatMapMut{e->f#e?{.then-> Lists#e; .else->{}} }
.flatMap f -> this.flatMapMut{e -> f#e .flow.list}
.peek f      -> this.map{e -> Block#(f#e, e)}
.mapFilter f -> this.map f .filter{o -> o.isSome}.map{o -> o!}
```
map and filter exist as separate methods for readability and performance.
map goes 1-1, so it preserves the size, and returning exactly 1 element avoids the 'sequence API' any flatMap implementation requires.
filter can not change the elements or their type, and can not add elements: useful restrictions for optimisations. Returning at most one element also avoids the 'sequence API'.

`.flatMapMut` desugars to an `actorMut`, using the `forEach` that passes a hygienic value to every call (see **forEach**):
```
.flatMapMut f -> this.actorMut[Void, R](Void, {down, _, e ->
  Block#(f#e .seqFlow.forEach(down, {d, x -> d.push#(d, x)}), Continue#)});
```

**Fenced expansions:**\
In most other approaches the lambda of `.flatMap` returns a flow, and this subtly interacts with fences/trickle down/lazy.
Our lambda returns a list, so it builds its whole expansion before emitting any element of it:
```
Lists#(Lists#("aa","bbb"), Lists#("c","poison","dd"), Lists#("e"))
  .flow
  .flatMap{ss -> ss.flow
    .map{s -> s == "poison" ? {.then -> Error.msg`poison`; .else -> s}}.list}
  .limit 3
  .map{s -> s.size}
  .list
```
This throws: building the expansion of the element at index 1 runs its whole nested flow, which meets `"poison"`.
With `.limit 2` it returns `Lists#(2, 3)`, since the element at index 1 is never pulled.
Where the lambda returns the inner flow instead, without the final `.list`, the same chain returns `Lists#(2, 3, 1)`: `.limit 3` stops the inner `map` before it pulls `"poison"`.

In our approach, any inner flow in the lambda is normal code, run in parallel when possible under the same rules as any other code.

### limit

`limit n` is the only stateful stage whose state is a counter. It is a decision point, but not the only one: every `actor` or `actorMut` whose lambda returns `Break#` is a decision point too (see **Decision points**).
Crucially `n` is not the result of a user lambda, so it is known before any stage starts: in
`es.flow.map{..}.limit 5 .list`
the terminal can preallocate a Java ArrayList of size exactly five
and run the map only on the 5 elements of es with the smallest indexes.
Over `ParRepr` and `ParPod` this is required, not an optimisation: running the stages on the whole source and stopping the tasks once the limit is full is positive speculation.

When the size is unknown, as after `.map{..}.filter{..}`, an upper bound is still known, so
under limit 50 we can run just 50 elements in parallel (possibly 5 tasks of 10), then decide how many more are needed.
After `.flatMap` or `.flatMapMut` not even an upper bound is known; after a fence the count is known again.
This matters most for Repr and IsoPod, where positive speculation is not allowed; elsewhere, avoiding unneeded positive speculation is just one option in the toolkit.
Pipeline parallelism can also be effective: its only shared mutable state is how much of the limit is left, or a simple binary STOP NOW.

`limit n` desugars to `.actorMut`, with the remaining budget as the actor state:
```
.limit n -> n == 0 ? {
  .then -> Error.msg`.limit 0 asks a whole chain of stages for no elements`;
  .else -> this.actorMut[mut Count[Nat], E](Count.nat n, {down, s, e -> Block#(
    down.push#(down, e),
    s--,
    s.get == 0 ? { .then -> Break#; .else -> Continue# })});
  }
```
Emit, decrement, stop on the element that exhausts the budget; element `n` is never pulled, so no stage runs on it.

`limit 0` throws. Most other approaches return the empty flow, but that is the mistake of the iconic functional `zip`: an answer manufactured so that the call can proceed rather than admit that something upstream is wrong. Asking for no elements while writing out a whole chain of stages signals a logic mistake. Since `n` is known before any element is pulled, a user wanting `n = 0` to give the empty flow can just put an `.if` around the flow call.
The exception is deterministic, and thrown where `.limit` is called rather than by the terminal: `n` is not a user lambda, so this does not weaken Lazy, and nothing has run yet, so nothing is left half done.
A deterministic exception fits here:
- The identity check over Repr and IsoPod has to be non deterministic, since a deterministic one would expose object identities (see **Modes**); this does not apply here.
- limit already throws deterministic exceptions for numbers too big.
A linter can not take this over, since `.limit x` may take `x` from outside.


### actor, actorMut, scan

`actor` and `actorMut` carry an actor state, `scan` an accumulator. In every case the call for one element can observe what the calls for the elements before it in flow order left behind, so the calls form a sequence, and the only parallel strategy for them is pipeline parallelism.
Long chains of stages may still compose nodes and data parallel regions.

The signatures of .actor and .actorMut are as follows:
```
mut .actor[S:*,R](state: iso S, f: read ActorImpl[S,E,R]): mut Flow[imm R];
mut .actorMut[S:*,R:*](state: iso S, f: read ActorImplMut[S,E,R]): mut Flow[R];
```
with
```
ActorImpl[S:*,E:*,R]:      { read #(down: mutH ActorSink[R], state: S,      e: E): mut ContinueBreak[Void] }
ActorImplMut[S:*,E:*,R:*]: { read #(down: mutH ActorSink[R], state: mutH S, e: E): mut ContinueBreak[Void] }
```

Actor and actorMut are conceptually the same operation, with different type signatures to cover more cases.
They are truly primitive: they have no desugaring.
They take an iso actor state and a lambda with three arguments: the sink, the current state and the current element.
Where the flatMap lambda returns a list of elements, this lambda pushes them on the sink.
This avoids allocating a container for them, and enables more parallelism: each element can start the next stage as soon as it is pushed, instead of waiting for a whole group to be computed.

The lambda returns `Continue#` or `Break#`, the same values `Block.loop` uses.
In a loop, `OuterReturn#v` skips the rest of the enclosing block and makes `v` its result; we use subtyping to ensure `OuterReturn#v` does not typecheck as the result of an `actor` lambda.

**Using the sink and the state:**\
A `mutH` binding can not be captured by any literal, so the sink, and in `actorMut` the state, appear only as receivers and arguments in the body of the lambda itself, never inside a nested literal: not in a branch of `? {..}`, not in a `.match{..}`, a `.thenDo{..}`, a `|> {..}` or a `.do{..}`. A step that pushes conditionally computes its decision first, as a `Bool` or a `mut Opt`, pushes with `pushOpt`, and answers `Continue#` or `Break#` from that decision; a helper method taking the sink and the state as `mutH` parameters gives a name to what the step needs twice, as the desugarings of `max`, `sortDistinct` and `distinct` do. The desugarings and the examples use
```
IfSome: { #[E:*](b: Bool, e: E): mut Opt[E] -> b ?[mut Opt[E]]{ .then -> Opts#e; .else -> {} } }
```

**A push after a `Break#`:**\
A stage after the actor may return `Break#` on an element the actor pushed while the actor call that pushed it is still running: the elements of a `flatMapMut` expansion after the one that fills a `limit`, or the code between two pushes. Nothing after that push can affect what the whole flow expression returns, so a push that a stage after answers with `Break#`, and every push after it, is a cancellation point: it stops the call, as `Yield#` does in a speculative call (see **Cancellation: `Yield#`**), and nothing after it runs, in every strategy, the sequential strategy included. This is what the `return` inside the loops of the pseudocode does (see **flatMap then flatMap**). An implementation that lets the call go on and drops its pushes runs code the sequential strategy never runs, and its exceptions and non termination become observable: with the actor ``{down, s, e -> Block#(down.push#(down, e), Error.msg`after`, Continue#)}`` before `.limit 1 .list`, the whole flow expression returns the list holding the element at index `0` and never throws `after`. The desugaring of `flatMapMut` relies on this: the `forEach` pushing the expansion is stopped at the push after the break, through the nested flow (see **Implementation pitfalls**). Under pipeline parallelism the actor node may be past the push when the break arrives: over `Par` that is positive speculation, with the usual holding; over `ParRepr` and `ParPod` it is not allowed, so an actor whose stage after may break runs in lockstep with it.

A push can not be inside `Repr.mutate` or `IsoPod.mutate`, where stopping the call would skip the invariant check and leave an object the caller keeps using half mutated (see **Implementation pitfalls**): the sink is `mutH` in both signatures, and a `mutH` reference can not be captured by the lambda passed to `mutate`, nor by any other.

**Why two different type signatures:**\
Many other approaches do 'something' if the sink is emitted as an element.
In Java, with gatherers, the results are unpredictable, depending on implementation details and differing between parallel and sequential flows.
Emitting the sink, or the mutable actor state, is highly counter intuitive, and even a consistent design for it would make parallelisation either unsafe or very limited.

If the emitted elements can be mut, the sink itself, or the mutable actor state, could be emitted as a mut element. In both signatures the sink is `mutH`: it can be pushed to and passed on, never stored, emitted or captured. The state is where the two differ:
- `actor` does not emit mut elements. Since mut can be assigned to read, read is blocked too and only imm survives, and the state is passed as it is. This simple restriction works in most cases.
- `actorMut` uses the harder to use but more expressive hygienic types: mut elements can be emitted, but the actor state is sealed as mutH too.
To push many elements, like every element of a list, the sink is passed to the `forEach` that takes a hygienic value: `xs.seqFlow.forEach(down, {d, x -> d.push#(d, x)})` (see **forEach**).

**scan:**\
`scan` is not a primitive: it desugars to an `actor` whose state is a `Var`:

`.scan(acc, f) -> this.actor[mut Var[imm S], S](Vars#[imm S](acc), {down, s, e -> Block#(s.set(f#(s.get, e)), down.push#(down,s.get), Continue#)})`

```
Flows#(1,2,3,4).scan(0, {acc, e -> acc + e}).list
```
returns `Lists#(1,3,6,10)`: the accumulator keeps the sum of the elements seen so far, and exactly one element, the current sum, is emitted per element pulled.

`.scan` offers a simpler signature than `.actor` but loses most of the actor flexibility.
Scan is a common concept in other approaches; the main differences are:
- The initial accumulator is not emitted. Where scan emits it too, as `scanl` in Haskell and `runningFold` in Kotlin do, `n` elements in give `n+1` out, and an empty flow gives one element. Here scan goes 1-1 like map, so a size or upper bound known before it is still known after it (see **limit**).
- The accumulator is `imm`. Where the state is mutable and the lambda may also skip or stop, as in Rust's `Iterator::scan`, scan is really an actor; here that is `.actor` and `.actorMut`.
- Scan is never parallel prefix. With an associative `f`, a parallel prefix (as in Java's `Arrays.parallelPrefix`) combines partial sums of chunks, calling `f` on pairs the sequential strategy never forms. Associativity is a logical but unenforced contract: a non associative `f`, or one that throws or does not terminate on one of those pairs, would give a different result in parallel, violating Flow unobservable parallelism. So scan is sequential, and running it in a node is its only parallelism.


**Example for actor:**\
Some other approaches support a 'window', for example processing a list of numbers through a sliding window of width 3:
```
Lists#(1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8).flow
  .actor[mut Var[List[Nat]], List[Nat]](Vars#[List[Nat]]{}, {down, s, e -> Block#(
    s.set(Trim#(s.get +> e, 3)),
    down.pushOpt#(down, Full#(s.get, 3)),
    Continue#
    )})
  .map{..}
  .list
Trim: { #(w: List[Nat], width: Nat): List[Nat] ->
  w.size > width ? { .then -> w.subList(1); .else -> w } }
Full: { #(w: List[Nat], width: Nat): mut Opt[List[Nat]] ->
  w.size == width ?[mut Opt[List[Nat]]]{ .then -> Opts#w; .else -> {} } }
```
Here map sees `[1,2,3]`; `[2,3,4]`; ...`[6,7,8]`.
The state is a `Var` of an imm `List[Nat]`, accumulating elements to emit them when the window is full; the window itself is imm, so `actor` suffices.


**Example for actorMut:**\
A list of mut heroes goes through the rooms of a dungeon, each room with its own state, monsters etc.
This runs with pipeline parallelism: the heroes loot rooms, kill monsters and sometimes die, each finding the rooms as the heroes before it in flow order left them, and we collect the
list of heroes that survive every room.

The room is what a mutH is typically made of: a large mutable graph whose leaves are imm.
The chests are a `mut EList[mut EList[Item]]`, the monsters and the graves are `ESet`s:
```
Room: {
  mut .fight(strength: Nat): Nat;
  mut .loot(strength: Nat): List[Item];
  mut .bury(grave: Opt[Grave]): Void;
  read .buried(name: Str): Bool;
  }
```
Every parameter of every `Room` method is imm, and so is every result.
So they can be called on a mutH room, as the state of an actorMut lambda is:
a mut method on a hygienic receiver takes only iso and imm arguments,
and a mut or read result comes back mutH or readH,
which can be called further but not stored or captured.
Inside the methods the receiver is plainly mut: consuming, filling and reading the leaves
is ordinary code.

The hero and the room meet through imm values:
```
Visit: { #(room: mutH Room, h: mut Hero): Void -> Block#(
  h.wound(room.fight(h.strength)),
  h.carry(room.loot(h.carryCapacity)),//zero if dead already
  room.bury(h.grave)//empty Opt is still alive
  ) }
Survivor: { #(room: readH Room, h: mut Hero): mut Opt[mut Hero] ->
  room.buried(h.name) ?{ .then -> {}; .else -> Opts#h } }

heroes.flow
  .actorMut[Room, mut Hero](room1, {down, s, h -> Block#(Visit#(s, h), down.pushOpt#(down, Survivor#(s, h)), Continue#)})
  .actorMut[Room, mut Hero](room2, {down, s, h -> Block#(Visit#(s, h), down.pushOpt#(down, Survivor#(s, h)), Continue#)})
  .list
```
`Room` can have no method taking the hero: the hero is mut.
`Visit` is not a method of the room: it takes the mutH room and the mut hero side by side
and nothing in it can store one into the other.
`Survivor` only reads the room, so it takes it readH, and a mutH room is one.


### map with context

`map(a, g)` desugars to `actorMut`: with `a` of type `iso ToIso[C]` and `g` of type `read F[iso C, E, R]`, it behaves exactly like
```
.map(a, g) ->
  this.actorMut[ToIso[C], R](a, {down, s, e ->
    Block#(down.push#(down, g#(s.iso.close, e)), Continue#)})
```
`map(a, g)` buys a simpler signature without affecting
optimisation/parallelisation: `actorMut` passes the state as `mutH S` together with the sink,
and asks for `Continue#` or `Break#`, while `map(a, g)` takes a lambda of two arguments
returning one element.

This relies on `ToIso` keeping encapsulation while producing a new version of the same conceptual data. If `ToIso` internally stores an `IsoPod`, moving the `iso` from one context to the next is easy, so a context can carry state forward.
The expression `s.iso.close` is very predictable: its only input is the `iso s`.
Thus we can run `s.iso.close` many times (sequentially, but it is likely very fast)
and run map with context with data parallelism, calling
`g#(_,e)` in parallel, each with its own `s.iso.close` result.
Care is needed to throw the same exceptions as the desugaring above.


### sort, sortDistinct, max, min

These four can not emit anything before consuming everything, so they are fences.
Their output is a finished sequence of known size, indistinguishable from a fresh source,
so a new data parallel region can start after it, unless the elements may share `mut` objects (see **Duplicated `mut` elements**):
`map f .sort by .limit 10000 .filter g .list`
is two data parallel regions with one fence in between.
A fence at the beginning or directly after another fence allows further optimisations.

`max` and `min` emit every element tied for the maximum (minimum), in flow order: `Lists#(3,1,3).flow.max{::}.list` is `Lists#(3,3)`.
`sort` is stable: elements that `by` ranks together keep their flow order.
All four take an `OrderBy`, not an `OrderHashBy`, so they never call `.hash`.
Under the sequential strategy the calls of `by` start only once every stage before the fence has finished with every element: placing each element as it arrives would let a call of `by` that does not terminate hide an exception thrown by a stage before the fence at a greater index. So, in every strategy, an exception of a stage before the fence propagates before any exception of `by`, whatever their indexes, and an exception of `by` propagates before any exception of a stage after the fence (see **example with a fence**).

**Desugaring:**\
min desugars exactly to max under the reversed order:
```
Rev: { #[T,K](by: OrderBy[T,K]): OrderBy[T] ->
  {t0, t1, m -> by#t0 <=> (by#t1, {.lt -> m.gt; .eq -> m.eq; .gt -> m.lt})} }

.min by -> this.max(Rev#by)
```
`Rev` swaps the outcome, not the arguments: `by#t0 <=> (by#t1, m')` computes the key of `t0` before the key of `t1`, as `by` itself does, so every call `by` receives is the same call, in the same order, in both.
Ties stay ties, so the same elements are emitted in the same flow order, and if a call of `by` throws or does not terminate, it is the same call.

max has a loose desugaring to sort: a stable sort under `Rev#by` emits the maximal elements before the others, in flow order, and an `actorMut` emits the run of elements tied with the one it pulls at the start:
```
.max by -> this.sort(Rev#by)
  .actorMut[mut Var[Opt[K]], E](Vars#[Opt[K]]{}, {down, s, e -> MaxStep#(down, s, by#e, e)})
MaxStep: { #[E:*,K](down: mutH ActorSink[E], s: mutH Var[Opt[K]], o: read Order[K], e: E): mut ContinueBreak[Void] ->
  KeyStep#(down, s, o, e, s.get.match{ .empty -> True; .some k -> o == k }) ? { .then -> Continue#; .else -> Break# } }
KeyStep: { #[E:*,K](down: mutH ActorSink[E], s: mutH Var[Opt[K]], o: read Order[K], e: E, keep: Bool): Bool -> Block#(
  s.set(Kept#(s.get, o, keep)),
  down.pushOpt#(down, IfSome#(keep, e)),
  keep) }
Kept: { #[K](old: Opt[K], o: read Order[K], keep: Bool): Opt[K] -> keep ? { .then -> Opts#(o.close); .else -> old } }
```
`KeyStep` records the key and pushes the element when `keep` holds, and returns `keep`; `sortDistinct` shares it.
The state holds only the key of the element pulled at the start, which is `imm`, so the elements themselves may be `mut`.
The key comes out of `by#e .close` as a `read K`, and a `read K` is not a `K`, even when `K` is `imm`: this desugaring, and those of `sortDistinct` and `distinct` below, do not typecheck as Fearless today, since the key can not be put into the `Opt[K]` or the `List[K]` of the state. Open decision: `Order[T].close`, and with it the `read T` that `OrderBy[T,K]#` receives, become `read/imm T`, so that the key of an `imm` `K` comes out `imm`; or the stages that keep keys stay primitives of the runtime, where a key is a Java object (see **Stage 1: Naive**). Until it is taken, these desugarings state the semantics and are not Fearless.
The result is the same when every call of `by` completes successfully, but an optimised implementation may make a different sequence of calls to `by`: a direct max needs about `n` comparisons where the sort needs about `n log n`, and the actor calls `by#e` again on the leading run. Thus, if some of those calls throw or do not terminate, this desugaring is not fully semantically accurate (while the desugaring of min is).

sortDistinct desugars to `.sort by` followed by collapsing each run of tied elements into the element of the run with the smallest index:
```
.sortDistinct by -> this.sort by
  .actorMut[mut Var[Opt[K]], E](Vars#[Opt[K]]{}, {down, s, e -> SortDistinctStep#(down, s, by#e, e)})
SortDistinctStep: { #[E:*,K](down: mutH ActorSink[E], s: mutH Var[Opt[K]], o: read Order[K], e: E): mut ContinueBreak[Void] ->
  Block#(KeyStep#(down, s, o, e, s.get.match{ .empty -> True; .some k -> o != k }), Continue#) }
```
The other candidates, `.distinct by .sort by` and `.sort by .distinct by`, do not typecheck when `by` is only an `OrderBy`: `.distinct` needs an `OrderHashBy`. With an `OrderHashBy`, a subtype of `OrderBy`, both typecheck, and return what `.sortDistinct by` returns when every call of `by` completes successfully.
Sorting before collapsing is what makes the hash unnecessary: after a stable sort the elements `by` ranks together are adjacent, so each element is compared only with the kept element before it in flow order, and the state is a single key rather than a set of all kept keys.
Since the sort is stable, the element kept for each class is the one with the smallest index, the same one `.distinct` keeps: `Lists#(3,1,3,2,1).flow.sortDistinct{::}.list` is `Lists#(1,2,3)`.
This desugaring sorts the duplicates too; a direct implementation can drop an element as soon as it compares equal to one already placed, for example while inserting into an ordered tree. That changes which calls to `by` are made, which is left under-specified as for `.sort`, provided the same choice is made in every strategy.

**How `.cmp` and `.hash` are called:**\
A `by` is `imm`, so its calls are observable only through which one, among those that throw or do not terminate, is made before the others, and through their cost. Which calls are made is under-specified, and must stay so: the implementation needs the freedom to pick its algorithms. The only requirement is the same choice in every strategy. Promises beyond that should only exclude truly pointless freedom, and should be worded loosely. Candidates:
- `by.cmp(e, e)` is never made: comparing an element with itself is pointless.
- `.hash` is not called on the same element many times: the hash can be stored next to the element, as a hash set does anyway.
- `.cmp` is called only on elements whose hashes are equal, for the operations taking an `OrderHashBy`, where comparing elements with different hashes is pointless.
- `.cmp(a, b)` is called with `a` before `b` in flow order. This only removes the dependence on the order of the two arguments; different algorithms still compare different pairs, so a `by` that is not antisymmetric or not transitive still gives different results under different algorithms.
- The number of calls stays within the usual complexity of the operation: about `n log n` for `sort`, about `n` for `max`, `min` and `distinct`.
The key `by#e` may be computed once per element and stored (decorate, sort, undecorate), which pays an expensive key, like a fitness, about `n` times instead of about `n log n` times; this changes the call sequence the desugarings above describe, where the key is computed inside every comparison, and is allowed by the under-specification.

**A `by` breaking its contract:**\
`sort`, `sortDistinct`, `max`, `min`, `distinct`, `mapping`, `set` and `eSet` may throw a non deterministic exception when the answers of `by` show that it breaks its contract: `.cmp` not antisymmetric or not transitive on the pairs compared, or two elements ranked together with different hashes. They do not have to: detecting it is part of the chosen algorithm, as the calls are, so it is fixed once, and made the same way in every strategy: a flow expression that throws it under one strategy throws it under every strategy, and one that does not throw it under one does not throw it under any. An algorithm may detect only what its own calls show, with no extra call made just to check.
Where they do not throw, the result is whatever the chosen algorithm returns with those answers, the same in every strategy.

### distinct

`mut .distinct[K](by: OrderHashBy[imm E,K]): mut Flow[E]`

`distinct` keeps, for each class of elements `by` ranks together, the one with the smallest index, in flow order: `Lists#(3,1,3,2,1).flow.distinct{::}.list` is `Lists#(3,1,2)`.
It is not a fence: each element is emitted as soon as it is found new.
Collecting the flow into a list and removing the repeated elements there makes it a fence: `.distinct by .first` would run the stages before it on every element, and `map f .distinct by .map g` would throw an exception of `f` at a greater index where the sequential strategy throws one of `g` at a smaller index.

**Desugaring:**\
distinct desugars to an `actorMut` whose state holds the keys kept so far:
```
.distinct by -> this.actorMut[mut Var[List[K]], E](Vars#[List[K]]{}, {down, s, e -> DistinctStep#(down, s, by#e, e)})
DistinctStep: { #[E:*,K](down: mutH ActorSink[E], s: mutH Var[List[K]], o: read Order[K], e: E): mut ContinueBreak[Void] ->
  DistinctPush#(down, s, o, e, s.get.flow.none{k -> o == k}) }
DistinctPush: { #[E:*,K](down: mutH ActorSink[E], s: mutH Var[List[K]], o: read Order[K], e: E, new: Bool): mut ContinueBreak[Void] -> Block#(
  s.set(Added#(s.get, o, new)),
  down.pushOpt#(down, IfSome#(new, e)),
  Continue#) }
Added: { #[K](ks: List[K], o: read Order[K], add: Bool): List[K] -> add ? { .then -> ks +> (o.close); .else -> ks } }
```
As for max, the state holds only `imm` keys, so the elements themselves may be `mut`, and as for max the key comes out of `by#e .close` as a `read K` (see the open decision under **sort, sortDistinct, max, min**).
This desugaring compares each element with every kept key and never calls `.hash`; a direct implementation keeps the keys in a hash set, calling `.hash` on each element and `.cmp` only on keys with the same hash. The hash is what `OrderHashBy` adds over the `OrderBy` of sortDistinct.
The result is the same when every call of `by` completes successfully; which calls to `by` are made is left under-specified as for `.sort`, provided the same choice is made in every strategy.

### ++

`mut ++ (b: read List[E]): mut Flow[read/imm E]`

`++` is a special fence: every element of the receiver goes through the stages before the `++` before `++` emits any element, the elements of `b` follow those of the receiver, and every element it emits is `read` or `imm`. `++` reads `b` when it emits its elements, after the stages before it have finished with every element: a stage before it may reach `b` through a `mut` element and change what `++` emits, the same in every strategy.
It is useful both in the modes and to start data parallelism after the fact.
After the `++` every element is `read` or `imm` and the stages before it have finished, so nothing the stages after it can reach is still being mutated: even if the receiver has no mode, the stages after the `++` can run data parallel on all the elements, of the receiver and of `b`.
This starts data parallelism after a stage run with the sequential strategy:
`persons.seqFlow.map{p->p.computeDNA}++{}.map{..}.list`
This is arguably not the most readable code, but appending the empty list gives type safe knowledge that the second map can be data parallel.

It is the fence, not the types alone, that makes this sound. Two persons of `persons` can share one `mut Address`, and in
```
persons.seqFlow.map{p -> Block#(p.moveTo(p.name), p)} ++ {} .map{q -> q.address.street}.list
```
trickling without the fence would read the street of the person at index 0 before the person at index 1 moves, while a parallel strategy could read it after that move. With the fence, both streets are read after every move, in every strategy.



### size, get, getOpt

`size` just desugars to `.list.size`, even where it could be faster, because
those optimisations are really brittle.
In many other approaches we would have
```
es.map.size//fast
es.map.filter.size//slow
```

`get` and `getOpt` desugar to `.list` in the same way:
```
.get -> this.list |> {xs -> xs.size == 1 ? {
  .then -> xs.getFirst;
  .else -> Error.msg(`Flow.get: the flow has `+(xs.size)+` elements, but .get needs exactly one`)}};
.getOpt -> this.list |> {xs -> xs.size <= 1 ? {
  .then -> xs.first;
  .else -> Error.msg(`Flow.getOpt: the flow has `+(xs.size)+` elements, but .getOpt needs at most one`)}};
```
The size is checked once every element has passed the whole chain, so
```
Lists#(1,2,3).flow.map{x -> x == 3 ? {.then -> Error.msg`three`; .else -> x}}.get
```
throws the exception `three`, not the exception about the size.
Deciding at the element at index 1 would make them short-circuit terminals, and every element with index greater than 1 would be positive speculation, which is not safe over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`.
As consuming terminals they are parallelised exactly like `.list`.
Implementing them as `.limit 2` followed by a check of the size is this short-circuit mistake: the example above would throw the exception about the size.

### mapping

`l.flow.map f .mapping(by, kem)`

Let `put` be what the terminal does with one element: `put#(x)` applies `kem.key` and `kem.elem`
to `x`, uses `by` to place the key among the keys already present, and throws when the key is
already present. Which calls `put` makes is left under-specified, as under `.sort`.
If there is a smallest index `j` such that `put#(f#ej)` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `put#(f#ej)` returns.
Otherwise the whole flow expression returns the map holding one entry per element, in flow order.

Two elements carrying the same key make `put` throw at the greater of the two indexes, so the whole flow expression
throws rather than keeping one value and dropping the other; in other approaches the same chain may silently keep either value.

**Parallelisation options:**

Data parallel:

`put` has two halves. Applying `kem.key` and `kem.elem` to `x` depends only on `x`, so it is elementwise and joins `map f` in one data parallel region: each task turns its chunk into key and element pairs.
Placing the keys can not be split: it is where a repeated key is found, and the calls it makes to `by` must be the same in every strategy, so they are the calls of placing the keys one at a time, in flow order, into a single map. Building one map per chunk and merging them would call `by` on different pairs, which is observable when `by` throws or does not terminate.
The placement then makes its calls one at a time, taking the pairs in flow order as the tasks finish them. An exception thrown by `f`, `kem.key` or `kem.elem` at index `j` is held until the placement arrives at `j`: if the placement finds a repeated key at a smaller index, that exception propagates instead, and the held exception is discarded (negative speculation).

Pipeline parallelism:

It can work as two nodes: one computes `map f`, `kem.key` and `kem.elem`, and one places the pairs into the map being built. The pairs naturally arrive in flow order.
The two overlap in time: the placing node places the element at index `j` while the other node computes the element at index `j+1`. The same holds when a stage whose calls form a sequence, such as an `actor`, comes before `.mapping`: the terminal's own per-element work overlaps the elements of that stage after `j` in flow order.
As usual, `Yield#` is needed to cancel speculative work that does not terminate, after an exception is thrown.

### forEach

forEach needs a mut lambda to make any sense, and in a Java stream that makes code like
```
v:Var[Nat]
Lists#(0,..,64).flow
  .map{_ -> v.get}
  .forEach{x -> Block#(acc.add x, v.set(v.get + 1))}
```
parallelism sensitive, preventing most parallelism.
In Fearless `.forEach` is a fence (see **Flow abstraction**), so that example behaves the same in every strategy (see **example with forEach**).
Receiving the elements in flow order is not enough: a `forEach` that runs its lambda on the element at index `j` as soon as it arrives, while tasks still run the stages on greater indexes, lets those stages read what the lambda writes, and in Java this is a data race. The fence waits until no task runs any stage (`m.list` or `m.join` in the pseudocode), except in the case below where nothing the lambda can mutate is visible to the stages.
The other terminals taking a lambda (`fold`, `foldAcc`, `mapping`, `set`, `eSet`) can not mutate anything that exists before the terminal: `fold` and `mapping` take `read` lambdas, `set` and `eSet` the `read #` of `AsImm`, and `foldAcc` mutates only the accumulator its `iso` lambda builds. They can read what they captured, as a stage can, so they are not fences (see **Consuming terminals**).

The fence is a semantic guarantee, and in plenty of situations it needs no materialisation:
```
Lists#(0,..,64).flow
  .map{..}//could capture v as before
  .forEach{..}
```
This needs materialisation only if the `map` lambda captures `v` as `read`.
The `onlyImmCapture` flag (see **Modes**) decides, with no need for `.immMap` or other methods taking `imm F` instead of `read F`:
with the flag on every lambda of the stages, and with source elements that are `imm`, as in mode `Par` over `imm` elements, nothing forEach can mutate is visible to the code executed as part of the stages, so forEach can run on each element, in flow order, as soon as the stages have finished with it.
Both conditions are needed:
- `read` source elements are not enough. With `m: mut List[mut Person]` and `r: read List[mut Person]` the same list, in `r.flow(Par#).map{p -> p.age}.forEach{a -> Block#(ages.add a, m.get(3).birthday)}`, with `ages` a fresh `mut EList[Nat]`, the forEach lambda captures `m`: running it on the element at index 0 before the map stage runs on index 3 changes the age `ages` holds at index 3.
- Without a mode the elements can share `mut` objects. Under `seqFlow` two persons can share one `mut Address`, as in the `++` example, and the forEach call on one person can mutate what the map stage reads on the other. Under `ParRepr`, the forEach lambda is exempt from the `onlyImmCapture` flag and can capture a `p: mut Repr[T]` that is also an element.
The forEach calls made this way are speculative calls: under the sequential strategy every call of every stage comes before the first forEach call, so a forEach call on the element at index `j` runs before a call of a stage at a greater index that may still throw or not terminate. As for every speculative call, its exception is held until every stage has finished with every element, and it never delays the exception of a stage: the forEach lambda runs in a node of its own, not on the calling worker, the node stops at its first exception and holds it, and the terminal returns as soon as an exception of a stage arrives, whatever the node is doing (see **example with forEach**). Running the forEach lambda on the calling worker as the elements arrive would throw its exception at index `2` where the sequential strategy throws the exception of a stage at index `5`, and would not return at all where that lambda does not terminate at index `2`. Any mutation such a call made is garbage collectable when the exception of the stage is caught.
This avoids materialising large ranges, as in
`0~~10000000 .flow.map{..}.forEach{..}`

`forEach` also has a version that passes a hygienic value to every call, as `Block.loop(a, body)` does:
```
mut .forEach[A](a: mutH A, f: mut ForEachBody[A,E]): Void;
ForEachBody[A,E:*]: { mut #(a: mutH A, e: E): Void }
```
With it an `actorMut` lambda can push every element of a list on its sink, as the desugaring of `.flatMapMut` does.

### let
Let is the most unusual operation: it declares a local variable in the middle of a flow.

```
es.flow.map{i -> Persons#(..)}
  .let stuff = {listUpToNow -> ..}
  .seqFlow
  .map{..}
  .list
```

`let` is a terminal, not a primitive:

```
.let(x, cont) -> this.list |>{xs->cont#(x#xs, xs)};
```
It consumes the flow into a list, passes that list to `x`, and passes
`cont` both the result of `x` and the same list.
The elements are needed twice and a flow consumes once, so they are materialised here.
Passing on the list rather than a flow makes that visible and allows parallelism:
the user calls `.flow` or `.seqFlow` on it.
What an optimising backend does with it, keeping this semantics, is in **example with let**.


### first, any, all, none, findFirst, findMap

These decide before the end, so under a parallel strategy every element pulled after the decision is positive speculation (the sequential strategy never pulls it).
Positive speculation is not safe for Reprs and IsoPods, since mutations made after the decision would be observable after the result.
Draining an `EList` into the flow does not help: it drops only the reference of the list, and a local or parameter such as `p: mut Repr[T]` may still hold an element that was never pulled.

**Desugaring:**\
None of these is primitive; they all desugar to `.limit 1`:
```
.first       -> this.limit 1 .getOpt
.findFirst f -> this.filter f .first
.findMap f   -> this.mapFilter f .first
.any f       -> this.filter f .first .isSome
.none f      -> this.any f .not
.all f       -> this.none{e -> f#e .not}
```
with `.mapFilter f` as in **map, filter, flatMap**.
`.limit 1` breaks right after pushing the element that arrives at it, so no other element is pulled, and `.getOpt` can not throw about the size since at most one element arrives at it.
On an empty flow the actor of `.limit 1` never runs, so `.first` is the empty `Opt`, `.any` is `False` and `.all` is `True`.
Composing `f` with `.not` changes no call and no order, so `.none f` throws the same exception at the same element as `.any f`, and `.all f` throws the same exception at the same element as `.any{e -> f#e .not}` (see **example with filter, under a short-circuit terminal**). `.all f` and `.any f` stop at different elements: `.any f` at the element where `f` completes successfully with `True`, `.all f` at the element where `f` completes successfully with `False`.

`.findFirst` returns `mut Opt[E]`, as `.first` does: the element may be `mut`.

**Decision points:**\
Decision points are the crux of positive speculation. A decision point is a call of an `actor` or `actorMut` lambda returning `Break#`: after it, no element is pulled, and the call of the stage before it that pushed the element is stopped at that push (see **A push after a `Break#`**). `limit` is the typical decision point because it desugars to such an `actorMut`; the short-circuit terminals and the loose desugaring of `max` do too.
Positive speculation is running code executed as part of a stage on an element before every decision point after that stage has decided whether that element is pulled.

An `actor` or `actorMut` node never runs out of order: it receives the elements in flow order, and its state can record that order. It can still run with positive speculation, ahead of a decision point after it:
```
l.flow
  .actorMut[mut Var[Nat], Nat](Vars#[Nat]0, {down, s, e ->
    Block#(s.set(s.get + e), down.push#(down, s.get), Continue#)})
  .limit 3
  .list
```
Under pipeline parallelism the `actorMut` node can make its call on the element at index 3 while the `limit` node has not yet received the element at index 2. The sequential strategy never makes that call: it is positive speculation.

The stages before an `actor` or `actorMut` that returns `Break#` can run with positive speculation too. In
```
l.flow.map f
  .actorMut[Void, Nat](Void, {down, _, e -> Block#(
    down.pushOpt#(down, IfSome#(e <= 5, e)),
    e > 5 ? { .then -> Break#; .else -> Continue# })})
  .list
```
the map stage can run with data parallelism, ahead of the `actorMut` node, and every call of `f` on an element after the break in flow order is positive speculation.

In both examples positive speculation is allowed in `Par` and not allowed in `ParRepr` and `ParPod`: there, no code executed as part of a stage runs on an element before every decision point after that stage has decided that the element is pulled.
A `limit` after only 1-1 stages (`map`, `peek`, `scan`, map with context) is no decision point for them: the canonical form moves it to the source (see **Canonical form**), so `rs.flow(ParRepr#).map f .limit n .list` needs no positive speculation and runs `f` on the `n` elements with the smallest indexes in parallel.

After a `.filter` the upper bound `limit 1` gives is a single element, so running more than one element at a time before a short-circuit terminal is always positive speculation: allowed in `Par`, not allowed in `ParRepr` and `ParPod` (see **example with filter, over ParRepr**).

### set, eSet

`set` and `eSet` take an `AsImm[E, E']`, whose `read #` turns each element into the `imm E'` the set holds, and an `OrderHashBy` placing it.
`.set(asImm, by)` returns what adding `asImm#ej` in flow order to an empty `Set` under `by` returns, and `.eSet(asImm, by)` the same over a fresh `mut ESet`: both are folds (see **Consuming terminals**), and the collection decides what adding an element `by` ranks together with one already present does. The flow order decides which element of each class arrives at the set before the others, so `.sort by .set(..)` reads the order (see **Ordering errors**).
The two halves of **mapping** apply: `asImm#ej` depends only on `ej`, so it is elementwise and joins the data parallel region before the terminal; the placement makes its calls of `by` one at a time, in flow order, with the placement algorithm fixed once (see **A `by` breaking its contract**). An exception of `asImm` at index `j` is held until the placement arrives at `j`, as the exceptions of `kem.key` and `kem.elem` are under **mapping**.

### fold, foldAcc, sum, join, last

`fold(acc, f)` builds the accumulator with `acc#` and calls `f` on it and each element in flow order: every call observes what the calls before it left in the accumulator, so the calls form a sequence. `fold` is the primitive consuming terminal, and its only parallelism is the node of **Consuming terminals**, fed by the stages before it.
The others desugar to it:
```
.foldAcc acc -> this.fold(acc, {a, e -> a.acc e}).result
.last        -> this.fold[mut Opt[E]]({{}}, {_, e -> Opts#e})
```
`sum(sn)` and `join(js)` are `sn.sumNumber this` and `js.joinStr this`: a fold written by the number and string types over the flow, `sum` with the checked `+` (see **Ordering errors** for which sums read the order).

### Consuming terminals

Apart from `forEach` and `let`, the consuming terminals are not fences: they trickle like the stages. The work of the terminal on the element at index `j` comes before any stage runs on the element at index `j+1`, as far as can be observed.
So a terminal with work of its own that can throw or not terminate is not `.list` followed by a loop. With `f` not terminating on `e1`, and `h` throwing on the first call,
`l.flow.map f .fold({0}, h)` throws: the sequential strategy calls `h` on `f#e0` and never pulls `e1`. `.list` followed by a fold calls `f#e1` first, and never returns.
The same holds for `foldAcc`, `mapping` (a repeated key throws), `set` and `eSet` (the calls of `by`), `sum` (the checked `+` throws on overflow) and `join`.
Under pipeline parallelism such a terminal is a node of its own: it places, adds or sums the element at index `j` while the stage before it works on the element at index `j+1` (see **mapping**).
The terminals with no work of their own that can throw or not terminate, `list`, `eList`, `size`, `get`, `getOpt` and `last`, can be `.list` followed by their work.

A trickling terminal fed through an iterator or any other pull that buffers breaks this when one element pulled becomes many elements, as a `flatMap` expansion or an `actor` pushing twice: the stages after the expansion run on all of it before the terminal sees its first element, so an exception of a stage at a greater index is thrown instead of the one of the terminal at a smaller index.

`fold` is the primitive consuming terminal: `list`, `eList`, `mapping`, `set`, `eSet`, `sum`, `join` and `last` desugar to a `fold` whose accumulator is a fresh mutable collection or number, and a `read` lambda can mutate its `mut` parameter:
```
.list -> this.fold({EList#[E]}, {acc, e -> Block#(acc.add e, acc)}).list
```
`isEmpty` is a consuming terminal, `.size == 0`: as `.first.isEmpty` it would be a short-circuit terminal, with positive speculation, not allowed over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`, for a question whose answer is known from the size of the source whenever no stage runs user code.

## Examples of combining operations

In all the examples `l` is `Lists#(0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7)`, with `ej` its element at index `j`, and `f` and `g` are of type `read F[Nat,Nat]`.

**Pseudocode:**\
Each example ends with the imperative code an optimising backend could generate for the whole flow expression, under the sequential strategy and under some parallel strategies.
The pseudocode is Java statements over Fearless expressions. Every lambda written in place in the flow expression is inlined, and the state of an actor becomes local variables; the lambdas the examples leave abstract, such as `f`, `g` and `by`, are called, as in `f#e`. `Opt#` is the empty `Opt`.
For example, with `ps` a `List[Person]`, `ps.flow.map{::.name}.filter{::.size > 3}.list` is
```
EList[Str] res= EList#;
for(Person p: ps){
  Str name= p.name;
  if(!(name.size > 3)){ continue; }
  res.add(name);
  }
return res.list;
```
The parallel strategies use a few primitives:
- `Mailbox[T]`: elements in flow order, possibly followed by one exception. `for(T x: m){..}` waits for each element as it arrives, and throws the exception when it arrives; `m.join` waits for the end of `m`, and throws its exception if any. `m.list` and `m.first` are sequential methods of `Mailbox`, with the obvious implementation in a comment where they first appear.
- `fork(Range c: Chunks#n){..}` splits the indexes from `0` to `n` into chunks and starts one task per chunk, running the block on some worker. The block sends elements with `out.send(x)`; when it throws, its exception is sent after them. `fork` returns the mailbox of the elements sent by the tasks, task after task in chunk order. `fork(List[T] c: Chunks#m){..}` does the same over the elements of the mailbox `m`, forming each chunk as its elements arrive; an exception arriving in `m` is sent after the elements of the tasks of the chunks before it.
- `node(in){x, out -> ..}` starts a node receiving the elements of `in`, a list or a mailbox, in flow order, and returns its mailbox `out`. The block runs on each element and sends with `out.send(x)`; it may update the local variables declared just before the node, which no other code uses. When the block throws, or an exception arrives, the node sends the exception and stops. Apart from those passed to methods of the elements, such as `Repr.mutate`, nodes are the only lambdas left in the pseudocode.
- `Mailbox#` is an empty mailbox the code itself feeds, with `feed.send(x)` and `feed.close`; a node reads it as any other mailbox.
- Leaving the whole flow expression, with `return` or with an exception, cancels through `Yield#` every task and node still running, and discards what they would send.
- `Sort#`, `Max#`, `MergeTree#` and `MaxTree#` are defined in **example with sort and limit: one merge tree for every strategy**. `KeySet#by` is a hash set placing its keys with `by.hash` and `by.cmp`; `s.add(x)` returns whether `x` was new. `IdentityCheck#rs` throws a non deterministic exception when two elements of `rs` are identity equal.

An exception arrives from a mailbox only after every element before it in flow order: this is how the pseudocode holds exceptions (see **Speculation**).
The flow expression above becomes, with data parallelism:
```
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(ps.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){
    Str name= ps.get(j).name;
    if(!(name.size > 3)){ continue; }
    out.send(name);
    }
  }.list;//EList[T] res= EList#; for(T x: this){ res.add(x); } return res.list;
```
and with pipeline parallelism:
```
Mailbox[Str] names= node(ps){p, out -> out.send(p.name)};
return node(names){name, out -> if(name.size > 3){ out.send(name); }}.list;
```

**example with size**\
`` l.flow.map{x -> Error.msg`boom`}.size ``

This code throws. In other approaches this may return the size without running the lambda, since the size is known.
`l.flow` enables mode `Par`, so this is an ordering error (see **Ordering errors**); the pseudocode below is what runs under `.suppressOrderingErrors`.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
Nat n= 0;
for(Nat e: l){
  Nat x= Error.msg`boom`;
  n= n + 1;
  }
return n;
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] ns= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  Nat n= 0;
  for(Nat j: c){
    Nat x= Error.msg`boom`;
    n= n + 1;
    }
  out.send(n);
  };
Nat n= 0;
for(Nat k: ns){ n= n + k; }
return n;
```
`x` is never read, but the call computing it stays, since it may throw or not terminate. No list is built: `.size` desugars to `.list.size`, but that list is not observable.

**example with a short-circuit terminal**\
`` l.flow.map{x -> x == 5 ? {.then -> Error.msg`five`; .else -> x}}.first ``

This code returns `Opts#(0)`; the exception of the element at index 5 is never thrown. Any strategy that runs the map lambda on the element at index 5 has to either discard that exception or throw a non deterministic exception.\
In other approaches a parallel strategy may non deterministically let that exception propagate.

The canonical form moves the `limit 1` of `.first` before the `map` (see **Canonical form**), so an optimising backend pulls the element at index `0` alone; the strategies below are those of a `first` after a stage a `limit` can not cross, as the `filter` of the next example, shown on this simpler chain.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
for(Nat e: l){
  if(e == 5){ Error.msg`five`; }
  return Opts#e;
  }
return Opt#;
```
Data parallel, with positive speculation, allowed in `Par`:
```
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){
    Nat e= l.get(j);
    if(e == 5){ Error.msg`five`; }
    out.send(e);
    break;
    }
  }.first;//for(T x: this){ return Opts#x; } return Opt#;
```
Each task stops after its first element: no other element of its chunk can come first. The task whose chunk starts at index 5, if any, throws, but in the mailbox that exception comes after `0`: the terminal returns before it arrives, and leaving cancels the tasks still running.

Pipeline parallelism:
```
return node(l){e, out -> if(e == 5){ Error.msg`five`; } out.send(e); }.first;
```

**example with filter, under a short-circuit terminal**\
`l.flow.filter{x -> P#x}.any{x -> Q#x}`\
with `P` and `Q` of type `read F[Nat,Bool]`.

The whole flow expression returns `True` if there is an index `i` such that:

- `P#ei && {Q#ei}` completes successfully with `True`
- forall `j<i`, `P#ej && {Q#ej}` completes successfully with `False`

It returns `False` if `P#ej && {Q#ej}` completes successfully with `False` for every index `j`.

If neither case applies, there is a smallest index `i` such that `P#ei && {Q#ei}` throws or does not terminate, and the whole flow expression returns what that expression returns.

This example shows clearly what "trickle the elements through the stages one at a time" means.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
for(Nat e: l){
  if(!P#e){ continue; }
  if(Q#e){ return True; }
  }
return False;
```
Data parallel, with positive speculation, allowed in `Par`:
```
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){
    Nat e= l.get(j);
    if(!P#e){ continue; }
    if(!Q#e){ continue; }
    out.send(e);
    break;
    }
  }.first.isSome;
```
Pipeline parallelism:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= node(l){e, out -> if(P#e){ out.send(e); }};
for(Nat e: m){
  if(Q#e){ return True; }
  }
return False;
```

**example with a fence**\
```
l.flow.map f .sort by .filter {::>3}.first
```
with `by` an `OrderBy[Nat]`.

Not an ordering error (see **Ordering errors**).

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns.\
Otherwise, if some calls of `by.cmp(...)` made by the sorting process throw or do not terminate, let `c` be the one the sorting process makes before the others: the whole flow expression returns what `c` returns.\
Otherwise, let `v0,...,vn` be the `f#ej` in `by` order, ties ordered by smallest index, and let `k` be the smallest number such that `{::>3}` applied to `vk` does not complete successfully with `False`.\
When no such `k` exists, the whole flow expression returns the empty `Opt`. When `{::>3}` applied to `vk` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what that call returns.\
Otherwise the whole flow expression returns `Opts#(vk)`.

Which calls to `by.cmp(...)` the sorting process makes, and in which order, is intentionally under-specified; the sorting process is under the control of the terminal. So `.sort by .first` could be optimised to just find the minimum element with the smallest index.

**Parallelisation options:**

Data parallel:

The map stage can run with data parallelism, and the sort can place each `f#ej` as it arrives.
The fence is about exception propagation, not about when code runs: no exception thrown by code executed as part of the filter stage propagates before every call of `f` and of `by.cmp` has completed successfully, and before the sort has fixed the flow order after it. The `f#ej` are `imm`, so a filter call on `f#ej` depends only on `f#ej`, and it can run as soon as `f#ej` is computed, while the sort is still running: positive speculation, allowed in `Par` (see **example with elementwise work after a fence**).
For example, if the filter call on `f#e6` throws, that exception is held. If `f#e2` then throws, and `f#e0` and `f#e1` complete successfully, the exception of `f#e2` propagates and the held exception is discarded. If every call of `f` and `by.cmp` completes successfully, the held exception propagates only if every filter call on the elements before `f#e6` in the flow order after the sort completes successfully with `False`.

After the fence the sorted sequence is finished and of known size, so the filter stage can run with data parallelism again. The terminal `.first` controls this, by running chunks of elements in flow order with positive speculation.

To preserve the observable behaviour, let `e` be the element, smallest in flow order, whose filter call does not complete successfully with `False`: the results of the filter calls on the elements after `e` in flow order are discarded. The call on `e` could complete successfully with `True`, throw or not terminate. If it does not terminate, it remains uncertain which element is `e`, so the other filter results can never be used.

Pipeline parallelism:
```
l.flow.map f .sort by .filter {::>3}.first
```
The map node emits elements; the sort node can use an algorithm like insertion sort to place them as they arrive one by one,
and must finish sorting before emitting anything.
The filter stage and the `first` terminal can be merged into a single
FilterFirst node that sends a cancel signal to the other nodes when it finds an element passing the filter.

Merging sort and filter, to sort fewer elements, is discussed in **example with sort and limit: one merge tree for every strategy**.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
for(Nat v: Sort#(vs, by, vs.size)){
  if(!(v > 3)){ continue; }
  return Opts#v;
  }
return Opt#;
```
The sort starts only once every call of `f` has returned: placing each element as it arrives would let a call of `by` that does not terminate hide an exception thrown by `f` at a greater index.

Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(l.get(j))); }
  };
MergeTree[Nat] s= MergeTree#(by, l.size);
for(Nat v: m){ s.add(v); }
for(Nat v: s.list){
  if(!(v > 3)){ continue; }
  return Opts#v;
  }
return Opt#;
```
Here the merges run while `f` runs, each as a task of its own, so they can not stop the calls of `f`.
Pipeline parallelism is the same code, with `Mailbox[Nat] m= node(l){e, out -> out.send(f#e)};`.

Merging sort and filter picks a different sorting algorithm, used then by every strategy; under the sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] ps= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){
  Nat v= f#e;
  if(v > 3){ ps.add(v); }
  }
for(Nat v: Sort#(ps, by, 1)){ return Opts#v; }
return Opt#;
```
With `k` of `1`, each merge makes at most one call of `by`: the sort is a search for the minimum.
The filter now runs on every element, as positive speculation. `{::>3}` over a `Nat` can not throw or fail to terminate, so running it inside the loop of `f` is not observable; with a filter that can, this merge is not legal (see **sort then filter** in **Extra interesting combinations of operators**).

**example with distinct, on a repeated element**\
```
Lists#(1, 1, 2).flow.map f
  .distinct by .filter {::>3}.list
```
with `by` an `OrderHashBy[Nat]`.

If there is a smallest index `i` such that `f#ei |> {e -> ...}` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ei |> {e -> ...}` returns. The body of that pipe `|>` places `e` among the values already kept and, when `e` is kept, applies `{::>3}` to `e`; the body makes calls like `by.hash(e)` and `by.cmp(e, f#ej)` with `j < i`, and which of them it makes is left under-specified, as under `.sort`.\
Otherwise the whole flow expression returns, in flow order, those of the kept values greater than `3`, where the kept values are decided only by the calls of `by`:
- `f#1` at index `0` is kept;
- `f#1` at index `1` is kept when `by` ranks it apart from the `f#1` at index `0`;
- `f#2` at index `2` is kept when `by` ranks it apart from every kept value.

**Warning: `by` is user code, and nothing forces it to rank two structurally equivalent values together.**
The two `f#1` are structurally equivalent, but a `by` whose `.cmp` answers `.lt` to every call keeps both of them. No reasoning in this document may assume that `by` respects the contract of an order (reflexive, antisymmetric, transitive): the contract is logical, not enforced. For a `by` breaking it, the operation either returns what its chosen algorithm returns or throws a non deterministic exception, the same in every strategy (see **A `by` breaking its contract**).

**Parallelisation options:**

Data parallel:

`map f` runs in chunks, and so can computing the key and hash of each result, which depend only on that element. Placing the keys can not be split: it is where a repeated key is found, so it runs sequentially, taking the results in flow order as the tasks finish them. The kept elements are given in chunks to `filter` tasks as soon as they are kept, without waiting for the `map` or `distinct` stage to finish, and `.list` joins the chunks in order.
An exception thrown at index `i`, by code executed as part of the map, distinct or filter stage, propagates only if nothing throws at a smaller index; otherwise it is discarded (negative speculation).

Pipeline parallelism:

A `map f` node, a `distinct` node and a `filter` node, the `filter` node also building the list. Each node receives the elements in flow order, so the `distinct` node needs no reordering: it keeps the kept keys as local mutable data, a hash set shared with no one, and emits each kept element at once. The `map f` node can also compute the keys, leaving the `distinct` node only the placing.

**Pseudocode:**\
with `xs` the list `Lists#(1, 1, 2)`.

Sequential strategy:
```
KeySet[Nat] kept= KeySet#by;
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat e: xs){
  Nat v= f#e;
  if(!kept.add(v)){ continue; }
  if(!(v > 3)){ continue; }
  res.add(v);
  }
return res.list;
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(xs.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(xs.get(j))); }
  };
KeySet[Nat] kept= KeySet#by;
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat v: m){
  if(!kept.add(v)){ continue; }
  if(!(v > 3)){ continue; }
  res.add(v);
  }
return res.list;
```
Pipeline parallelism is the same code, with `Mailbox[Nat] m= node(xs){e, out -> out.send(f#e)};`.

Data parallel, with the filter in tasks of its own:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(xs.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(xs.get(j))); }
  };
KeySet[Nat] kept= KeySet#by;
Mailbox[Nat] ks= node(m){v, out -> if(kept.add(v)){ out.send(v); }};
return fork(List[Nat] c: Chunks#ks){
  for(Nat v: c){
    if(!(v > 3)){ continue; }
    out.send(v);
    }
  }.list;
```

**Choosing by cost:**\
Both versions are correct. Which one is faster depends on the cost of the filter compared with the cost of sending elements to a task, and the flow expression does not state it.
Every strategy is observably equal, so a backend may decide with any information, even information that changes between runs or during one run, provided the under-specified choices, such as which calls of `by` are made, stay the same:
- Static estimate: after inlining, the body of each stage is visible code. `v > 3` is one comparison, with no call to unknown code, no loop and no allocation: it costs less than sending the element to a task, so it runs in the loop of the stage before it. A body calling an abstract lambda such as `f`, or a method with loops or recursion, has an unknown cost.
- Hints: the user states the cost, as in **Chunk sizes**.
- Measurement: the generated code holds both versions, and the runtime times the first elements of each stage, then picks a version and adapts the chunk sizes as it goes. `allTheCoresBusy` is the extreme case of this.
- Profiles: measurements of previous runs, stored per flow expression, pick the version at the next run.
A static estimate settles the clear cases, like this filter; measurement settles the others.

**example with limit**\
`l.flow.map f .limit 3 .map g .list`

If there is a smallest index `j` among `0`, `1` and `2` so that the expression `g#(f#ej)` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `g#(f#ej)` returns. Otherwise the whole flow expression returns `Lists#(g#(f#e0), g#(f#e1), g#(f#e2))`.

The result does not depend on `f#ej` for any `j` above `2`, so an exception thrown by `f#e5` or `g#(f#e5)` is discarded.\
In other approaches a parallel strategy may non deterministically let that exception propagate.

**Canonical form:**\
Before generating code, a backend can rewrite the chain into a canonical form. Each rewrite keeps every call and the order of the calls, so the whole flow expression returns the same for every `f` and `g`, including those that throw or do not terminate:
- `.map f .limit n` is `.limit n .map f`. `map` goes 1-1, and `.limit n` breaks right after pushing its `n`th element, so both make the calls of `f` on the elements at the first `n` indexes, each before the calls of the stages after it on the same element.
- `.limit n .limit m` is `.limit(n.min(m))`; written in a row, the two limits are an ordering error (see **Ordering errors**).
- `.map f .map g` is `.map{x -> g#(f#x)}`, and `.filter p .filter q` is `.filter{x -> p#x && {q#x}}`.
- `.limit n` right after a list source of known size is the source cut to its first `n` elements.

The canonical form moves each `limit` as close to the source as these rewrites allow, and fuses what is left. A `limit` can not move before a `filter`, `flatMap`, `flatMapMut`, a fence, or an `actor` or `actorMut` whose lambda may push other than once or return `Break#` (see **scan then limit**): they change which elements arrive at it. `.filter p .map f` can not become `.map f .filter{..}` either: `f` would run on the elements `p` rejects, and its exceptions and non termination would be observable.
The canonical form of this example is `l.flow.limit 3 .map{x -> g#(f#x)}.list`: the limit becomes part of the source, and the pseudocode is generated from what is left. `l.take(3)` is the list of the first `3` elements of `l`, or all of them when `l` is shorter.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat e: l.take(3)){ res.add(g#(f#e)); }
return res.list;
```
Data parallel:
```
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size.min(3))){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(g#(f#(l.get(j)))); }
  }.list;
```
Pipeline parallelism:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= node(l.take(3)){e, out -> out.send(f#e)};
return node(m){v, out -> out.send(g#v)}.list;
```
No strategy uses positive speculation: no element after index `2` is ever pulled.

**example with actorMut**\
```
l.flow
  .map f
  .actorMut[mut Var[Nat], Nat](Vars#[Nat]0, {down, s, e ->
    Block#(s.set(s.get + e), down.push#(down, s.get), Continue#)})
  .map g .list
```

Let `h` be the actor lambda, `s` the state built by `Vars#[Nat]0`, `down` the sink that applies `g` to each value pushed on it, and `sj` the sum of `f#e0` up to `f#ej`. If there is a smallest index `j` such that `h#(down, s, f#ej)` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `h#(down, s, f#ej)` returns.\
Otherwise the whole flow expression returns the list of `g#s0` to `g#s7`.

**Parallelisation options:**

The actor can not be split: the call for one element observes what the calls for the elements before it in flow order left in the state.

Data parallel:

`map f` and `map g` run in chunks, with the actor making its calls one at a time between the two data parallel regions. The regions still overlap in time: no fence is needed, since the actor emits one element at a time, and each chunk of its output goes to a `map g` task as soon as it is full.
One cost remains: the actor needs the elements in flow order, so the results of `map f` are put back in flow order before the actor sees them; parallelising only chunks of limited size bounds the reordering buffer.

Pipeline parallelism:

This is the natural strategy for an `actorMut`: a `map f` node, the actor node and a `map g` node that also builds the list. The actor node owns `s` as local mutable data and receives the elements in flow order, so there is no reordering buffer. Several actor stages in a row each become a node, running pipeline parallel with each other.
The cost is that `map f` and `map g` get one node each. The two strategies compose: a `map` node can be replaced by a data parallel region feeding the actor node in order, which is the data parallel version above.

In both strategies, an exception thrown at index `j` propagates only if nothing throws at a smaller index; the work on greater indexes is negative speculation, and the state `s` it mutated is garbage collectable once the exception is caught.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
Nat s= 0;
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){
  s= s + f#e;
  res.add(g#s);
  }
return res.list;
```
The `Var` of the actor state becomes the local `s`: it is `iso`, so nothing else can reach it.

Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] vs= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(l.get(j))); }
  };
Nat s= 0;
Mailbox[Nat] ss= node(vs){v, out -> s= s + v; out.send(s); };
return fork(List[Nat] c: Chunks#ss){
  for(Nat x: c){ out.send(g#x); }
  }.list;
```
The actor node receives the results of `map f` in flow order: the mailbox `vs` is the reordering buffer.
An exception of `f` at index `j` arrives at the actor node after the elements before it, is sent on after their sums, and arrives at the terminal after the results of `g` on those sums: it propagates only if no call of `g` before it in flow order throws, as under the sequential strategy.

Pipeline parallelism:
```
Mailbox[Nat] vs= node(l){e, out -> out.send(f#e)};
Nat s= 0;
Mailbox[Nat] ss= node(vs){v, out -> s= s + v; out.send(s); };
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat x: ss){ res.add(g#x); }
return res.list;
```

**example with let**\
```
l.flow
  .map f
  .let[Nat, mut List[Nat]] total = {xs -> xs.flow.sum 0}
  .flow
  .map{n -> n + total}
  .list
```

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns.\
Otherwise, let `total` be the checked sum of the `f#ej` over every index `j`, in flow order: if it overflows, the whole flow expression returns that exception. Otherwise, if there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej + total` overflows, it returns that exception; otherwise it returns the list holding `f#ej + total` at each index `j`.

The result of every `f#ej` is needed twice, so `let` materialises the flow up to that point as a list.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] xs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ xs.add(f#e); }
Nat total= 0;
for(Nat x: xs){ total= total + x; }
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat x: xs){ res.add(x + total); }
return res.list;
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(l.get(j))); }
  };
List[Nat] xs= m.list;
Nat total= 0;
for(Nat x: xs){ total= total + x; }
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(xs.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(xs.get(j) + total); }
  }.list;
```
The sum runs only once every call of `f` has returned, as `let` passes the whole list to its lambda.

With `{xs -> xs.flow.fold({0}, {a, x -> a.aluAddWrap x})}` in place of `{xs -> xs.flow.sum 0}`, the sum can not throw or fail to terminate, so it runs inside the loop of `f`, and the loop computing `total` disappears:
```
EList[Nat] xs= EList#;
Nat total= 0;
for(Nat e: l){
  Nat x= f#e;
  xs.add(x);
  total= total.aluAddWrap(x);
  }
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat x: xs){ res.add(x + total); }
return res.list;
```
The checked `+` of `.sum` throws on overflow, so inside the loop of `f` its exception would have to be held until every call of `f` has returned.

**example with forEach**\
`l.flow.map{_ -> v.get} .forEach{x -> Block#(acc.add x, v.set(v.get + 1))}`\
with `v` a fresh `mut Var[Nat]` holding `0`, and `acc` a fresh `mut EList[Nat]`.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `v.get |> {x -> Block#(acc.add x, v.set(v.get + 1))}` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `v.get |> {x -> Block#(acc.add x, v.set(v.get + 1))}` returns.\
Otherwise the whole flow expression returns `Void`, and afterwards `acc` holds eight times `0` and `v` holds `8`.

The `map` lambda reads `v` and the terminal lambda writes `v`. Crucially `.forEach` is a fence.

In many other approaches forEach is not a fence, and `acc` may end up holding `0` to `7` instead. Fearless flows need the fence to allow parallelism: by its nature, `.forEach` performs side effects on mut captured values.

With the fence the map stage can run with data parallelism: every `v.get` reads the same `0`, and no code executed as part of a stage reads what the terminal writes, since the terminal runs after the fence and forEach is the only terminal that can mutate existing data.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] xs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ xs.add(v.get); }
for(Nat x: xs){
  acc.add(x);
  v.set(v.get + 1);
  }
return Void;
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(v.get); }
  };
List[Nat] xs= m.list;
for(Nat x: xs){
  acc.add(x);
  v.set(v.get + 1);
  }
return Void;
```
The `map` lambda captures `v`, so the forEach lambda can not run before every task has finished: it would change what the tasks still running read.
With `{_ -> 0}` in place of `{_ -> v.get}` the `map` lambda carries the `onlyImmCapture` flag, and with `l` an `imm List` the forEach lambda runs as the elements arrive, and `xs` is not needed (see **forEach**):
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(0); }
  };
Mailbox[Nat] feed= Mailbox#;
Opt[Info] held= Opt#;
Mailbox[Void] done= node(feed){x, out ->
  if(held.isSome){ return; }
  try{ acc.add(x); v.set(v.get + 1); }catch(Info i){ held= Opts#i; }
  };
for(Nat x: m){ feed.send(x); }
feed.close;
done.join;
if(held.isSome){ throw held!; }
return Void;
```
The forEach lambda runs in a node, in flow order, as the sequential strategy runs it. The node keeps its own exception and skips the elements after it, so that an exception of the `map` lambda at a greater index, which the sequential strategy meets first, wins; the calling worker reads `m` itself, so that such an exception leaves the whole flow expression at once, also while the node is inside a forEach call that does not terminate; and the terminal waits for the node only once every call of the `map` lambda has returned. With `{_ -> 0}` the calls of the `map` lambda can not throw, so here the node is only cheaper than `xs`; with a `map` lambda that can, the three precautions are what keeps the strategy observably equal to the fence.

**example with filter, over ParRepr**\
```
rs.flow(ParRepr#)
  .filter{r -> r.mutate{
    c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}
    > 3}
  .size
```
If any two `Repr`s (or `IsoPod`s in an equivalent example) are identity equal, a non deterministic exception is thrown.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `rj.mutate{c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `rj.mutate{c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}` returns. Thanks to strong exception safety we will never be able to observe how many elements in the flow/list have been mutated.\
Otherwise the whole flow expression returns the number of indices `j` where `rj` holds more than `3` after being increased, and afterwards every `rj` in `rs` holds one more than `rj` held before the flow.

Every element is increased, the rejected elements included, and `rs` still holds every element, so the increases are readable once the whole flow expression has completed successfully.

The filter stage can run with data parallelism while `size` is the terminal.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
IdentityCheck#rs;
Nat n= 0;
for(mut Repr[Count[Nat]] r: rs){
  Nat x= r.mutate{i -> Block#(i++, i.get)};
  if(!(x > 3)){ continue; }
  n= n + 1;
  }
return n;
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] ns= fork(Range c: Chunks#(rs.size)){
  Nat n= 0;
  for(Nat j: c){
    Nat x= rs.get(j).mutate{i -> Block#(i++, i.get)};
    if(!(x > 3)){ continue; }
    n= n + 1;
    }
  out.send(n);
  };
IdentityCheck#rs;
Nat n= 0;
for(Nat k: ns){ n= n + k; }
return n;
```
The identity check runs on the calling worker while the tasks run, and completes before `ns` is read: when two tasks race on one `Repr`, the non deterministic exception is thrown whatever the race did.
The sequential strategy runs the identity check too: it is not a consequence of racing, and skipping it would make the strategy observable.
The lambdas are written in place, so the `onlyImmCapture` flag is checked when compiling and leaves no code.

Consider the same example but with a short-circuit terminal like `.any` in place of `size`
```
rs.flow(ParRepr#)
  .filter{r -> r.mutate{
    c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}
    > 3}
  .any {r->r.look{..}}
```
In many other approaches the parallel strategy uses positive speculation, and the increases on elements after the decision survive the whole flow expression.
We can not accept this.
This is exactly code screaming 'parallel' that is only safe to execute sequentially,
so it throws a non deterministic exception, even when the optimiser would 'want' to run it sequentially.

The only sound option is to insert a fence before
any operation that would need positive speculation to run in parallel:
```
rs.flow(ParRepr#)
  .filter{r -> r.mutate{
    c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}
    > 3}
  .sort {..}
  .any {r->r.look{..}}
```
Overall, parallelising `Repr`s and `IsoPod`s is about
- running an identity check
- checking the `onlyImmCapture` flag on the lambdas of the flow
- avoiding positive speculation with no fence before it, and using the sequential strategy where positive speculation after a fence would be needed, unless the elements arriving at the fence are `read` or `imm` in the sense of **Ordering errors**: the fence is a `++`, or a `++`, a `flatMap`, an `actor` or a `scan` comes before it (see **example with `++` as a fence before positive speculation, over ParRepr**). The chain records no element type, so a `map` returning an `imm` value does not count; a `map` after such an operation may create `mut` objects, but they reach no `Repr` or `IsoPod` of the source, so the stages after the fence can not mutate anything that exists before the flow
- behaving exactly as if a flow in mode `Par` created a mut object in the map stage after the source: those muts are isolated from each other and from what the lambdas captured, so they can not interact until a `flatMapMut` or an `actorMut` duplicates them (see **Duplicated `mut` elements**). Depending on the stages, data parallelism or pipeline parallelism could be more appropriate.

**example with sort and limit: one merge tree for every strategy**\
`l.flow.map f .sort by .limit 3 .list`\
with `by` an `OrderBy[Nat]`.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns.\
Otherwise, if some calls of `by.cmp(...)` made by the sorting process throw or do not terminate, let `c` be the one the sorting process makes before the others: the whole flow expression returns what `c` returns.\
Otherwise, let `v0,...,v7` be the `f#ej` in `by` order, ties ordered by smallest index: the whole flow expression returns `Lists#(v0, v1, v2)`.

The calls of the sorting process are under-specified, but must be the same in every strategy. Inserting each element as it arrives suits pipeline parallelism, merging sorted chunks suits data parallelism, and sorting the whole list suits the sequential strategy; the three make different calls, so picking one per strategy would make the strategy observable whenever a call of `by` throws or does not terminate.

One choice serves every strategy: a stable merge sort over a tree fixed by the indices alone. Blocks `(0,1)`, `(2,3)`, `(4,5)`, `(6,7)` are merged, then `(0..1,2..3)` and `(4..5,6..7)`, then `(0..3,4..7)`; when the size is not a power of two, the blocks left over when the element with the greatest index arrives are merged from the right. The tree depends on the size alone, not on chunk sizes or on the number of workers.
The merges have a fixed order: each runs as soon as both its blocks are finished, as in a binary counter, so when the element at index `7` arrives the merges `(6,7)`, `(4..5,6..7)` and `(0..3,4..7)` run in this order.
- Sequential strategy: the merges run in this order once every `f#ej` is computed; merging as the elements arrive would let a call of `by` that does not terminate hide an exception thrown by `f` at a greater index.
- Pipeline parallelism: the sort node places each element as it arrives, as **Flow abstraction** allows a fence to do, running each merge as a task of its own, so that a call of `by` that does not terminate does not stop the node from receiving an exception thrown by `f`.
- Data parallelism: a merge depends only on its two blocks, so a task can run any merge whose blocks are finished, on any worker. An exception thrown by a call of `by` is held until every `f#ej` and every call the sequential strategy makes before it have returned.
- Every merge compares an element of its left block with one of its right block, so in every `.cmp(a, b)` call `a` is before `b` in flow order (see **sort, sortDistinct, max, min**).

With `.limit 3` after the sort, a merge stops once it has emitted three elements: the rest of the merged block can not be among those three. Its calls are a prefix of those of the full merge, at most three per merge, with one merge fewer than the elements, and no block holds more than three elements. For a `by` respecting its contract the result is unchanged; for any other `by` the truncated tree is simply the chosen algorithm. The limit is known before any element is pulled, so the choice is the same in every strategy.

The same reasoning applies to merging `sort` with a stage after it. In **example with a fence**, `.sort by .first` may become a search for the minimum, and `.sort by .filter{::>3}.first` may sort only the elements passing the filter. Both change which calls of `by.cmp` are made, and both are allowed, like any other sorting algorithm, provided the choice is the same in every strategy. Sorting only the elements passing the filter also runs the filter on elements the sequential strategy may never filter: this is positive speculation, so the exceptions of the filter are held until the sort has fixed the flow order of their elements, and it is not allowed over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`.

The same tree serves `max` and `min`: a merge compares the front elements of its two blocks and keeps the leading run of the winner, or both runs in order on a tie: one call per merge, seven over `l`. For `sortDistinct`, a merge drops the element of the right block of each pair ranked together.

**Pseudocode:**\
`Sort#(vs, by, k)` calls `sort`, the merge tree under the sequential strategy, each merge keeping at most `k` elements:
```
List[Nat] sort(List[Nat] vs, OrderBy[Nat] by, Nat k){
  if(vs.isEmpty){ return vs; }
  EList[List[Nat]] blocks= EList#;
  for(Nat j: vs.indexes){
    List[Nat] b= Lists#(vs.get(j));
    for(Nat n= j; n % 2 == 1; n= n / 2){ b= merge(blocks.pop, b, by, k); }
    blocks.push(b);
    }
  while(blocks.size > 1){
    List[Nat] b= blocks.pop;
    blocks.push(merge(blocks.pop, b, by, k));
    }
  return blocks.pop;
  }
List[Nat] merge(List[Nat] a, List[Nat] b, OrderBy[Nat] by, Nat k){
  EList[Nat] res= EList#;
  Nat i= 0;
  Nat j= 0;
  while(res.size < k && i < a.size && j < b.size){
    if(by.cmp(a.get(i), b.get(j)).isGt){ res.add(b.get(j)); j= j + 1; continue; }
    res.add(a.get(i));
    i= i + 1;
    }
  while(res.size < k && i < a.size){ res.add(a.get(i)); i= i + 1; }
  while(res.size < k && j < b.size){ res.add(b.get(j)); j= j + 1; }
  return res.list;
  }
```
Adding the element at index `j` runs one merge per trailing `1` in the binary digits of `j`: the binary counter. The left block is popped after the right one, so `a` is before `b` in flow order.
`Max#(vs, by)` is the same tree with this merge in place of `merge`:
```
List[Nat] maxMerge(List[Nat] a, List[Nat] b, OrderBy[Nat] by){
  Ord o= by.cmp(a.get(0), b.get(0));
  if(o.isGt){ return a; }
  if(o.isLt){ return b; }
  return a ++ b;
  }
```
`MergeTree#(by, k)` makes the calls of `Sort#`, with each merge running as a task of its own as soon as its two blocks are complete. `s.add(x)` adds `x` as the element at the next index; it never waits for a call of `by` and never throws. `s.list` merges the blocks left over from the right, waits for the merges in the order `Sort#` makes them, and returns the result: if some calls of `by` throw or do not terminate, it returns what the one `Sort#` makes before the others returns. `MaxTree#by` is the same for `Max#`.

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return Sort#(vs, by, 3);
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(l.get(j))); }
  };
MergeTree[Nat] s= MergeTree#(by, 3);
for(Nat v: m){ s.add(v); }
return s.list;
```
The loop over `m` adds the elements in flow order, whatever the order in which the tasks finish; the merges of the blocks of different chunks run while the tasks of the chunks after them are still running.
Pipeline parallelism is the same code, with `Mailbox[Nat] m= node(l){e, out -> out.send(f#e)};`.

**example with elementwise work after a fence**\
`l.flow.map f .sort by .map g .list`\
with `by` an `OrderBy[Nat]`.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns.\
Otherwise, if some calls of `by.cmp(...)` made by the sorting process throw or do not terminate, let `c` be the one the sorting process makes before the others: the whole flow expression returns what `c` returns.\
Otherwise, let `v0,...,v7` be the `f#ej` in `by` order, ties ordered by smallest index. If there is a smallest number `k` such that `g#vk` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `g#vk` returns. Otherwise the whole flow expression returns the list of `g#v0` to `g#v7`.

The fence puts every call of `g` after every call of `by`, but the elements are `imm`: `g` can not change what `by` reads, and `g#vk` depends only on `vk`, not on its place in flow order.
So `g` can run on each `f#ej` in parallel with the sort rather than after it; the sort carries each result next to its element. The calls of `g` do not run in the tasks of `f`: a call of `g` that does not terminate would stop the calls of `f` after it in that task, and one of those may throw.
Only the exceptions of `g` are held until the fence: the place in flow order of `f#ej` after the sort is known only when the sort finishes, so an exception thrown by `g` is held until then, and propagates only if no call of `f` or of `by`, and no call of `g` on an element before it in flow order, throws or does not terminate. A call of `g` that does not terminate is cancelled through `Yield#` when one of those throws.

This needs the elements arriving at the fence to be `read` or `imm`. In
`numbers.flow.map{n -> Persons#n}.sort{p -> p.age}.map{p -> Block#(p.birthday, p)}.list`
the `map` after the fence mutates what `by` reads, and running it early would change the order.

The same holds before a short-circuit terminal, as in **example with a fence**: the filter can run on every element while the sort proceeds, as positive speculation allowed in `Par`. Unlike merging sort and filter to sort fewer elements, this leaves the calls of `by` unchanged.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat v: Sort#(vs, by, vs.size)){ res.add(g#v); }
return res.list;
```
Data parallel, running `g` while the sort runs:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(l.get(j))); }
  };
List[Nat] vs= m.list;
MergeTree[Nat] s= MergeTree#(by, vs);
for(Nat j: vs.indexes){ s.add(j); }
Slots[Nat] ws= Slots#(vs.size);
Mailbox[Nat] gs= fork(Range c: Chunks#(vs.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ ws.set(j, g#(vs.get(j))); }
  };
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat j: s.list){
  Opt[Nat] w= ws.get(j);
  if(w.isSome){ res.add(w!); continue; }
  res.add(g#(vs.get(j)));
  }
return res.list;
```
Here `MergeTree#(by, vs)` sorts indexes, comparing the elements of `vs` at those indexes, and `Slots#n` holds an `Opt` per index, empty until a task sets it.
Nothing ever reads `gs`, so no exception of `g` is held: a slot left empty, because its call threw, did not terminate or has not run yet, is computed again by the loop over the sorted indexes, which calls `g` in the flow order after the sort, as the sequential strategy does. By Determinism the call returns what the call in the task returned, and leaving cancels the tasks still running.
Each chunk task stops at its first exception, leaving empty the slots after it; the loop computes them.

**example with two max**\
`l.flow.map f .max by1 .max by2 .list`\
with `by1` and `by2` of type `OrderBy[Nat]`.

Not an ordering error (see **Ordering errors**).

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns.\
Otherwise, if some calls of `by1` made by the max tree throw or do not terminate, it returns what the one the tree makes before the others returns; every call of `by2` comes after every call of `by1`, since the `max by2` stage receives nothing until the `max by1` stage has finished (see **sort, sortDistinct, max, min**).\
Otherwise the `max by1` stage keeps the elements tied for the maximum under `by1`, in flow order; the `max by2` stage keeps, among those, the ones tied for the maximum under `by2`. When every call of `by1` and `by2` completes successfully, this is one `max` under the order that compares with `by1` and breaks ties with `by2`.
That single pass is not a legal replacement: it calls `by2` on two elements as soon as `by1` ties them, and an element after both in flow order may beat both under `by1`. Such elements never arrive at the `max by2` stage, so a call of `by2` on them that throws or does not terminate changes the result.
Compare `.filter p .filter q`, which is exactly `.filter{e -> p#e && {q#e}}`: `&&` calls `q` only on the elements `p` accepts, known at once. Whether an element passes the `max by1` stage is known only when the flow ends.

What is legal:
- The `max by1` stage materialises only its current leading run, not the whole flow, and the `max by2` stage runs on that run alone once the flow ends. With the tree of **example with sort and limit: one merge tree for every strategy**, the `max by1` stage runs with data parallelism too.
- The single pass as positive speculation, allowed in `Par`: `by2` runs on the current leading run as the elements arrive, and its exceptions are held until the `max by1` stage finishes and confirms the run; calls on elements that left the run are discarded, or cancelled through `Yield#`.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return Max#(Max#(vs, by1), by2);
```
Data parallel:
```
Mailbox[Nat] m= fork(Range c: Chunks#(l.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ out.send(f#(l.get(j))); }
  };
MaxTree[Nat] s= MaxTree#by1;
for(Nat v: m){ s.add(v); }
return Max#(s.list, by2);
```
The `max by2` stage runs only on the leading run `s.list` returns, so no call of `by2` is made on an element the `max by1` stage drops.

**example with `++` as a fence before positive speculation, over ParRepr**
```
rs.flow(ParRepr#)
  .map{r -> Block#(r.mutate{i -> i.evaluate}, r)}
  ++ {}
  .any{r -> r.look{i -> i.fitness > 90}}
```
with `rs` a `mut List[mut Repr[Individual]]` and `Individual` as in the genetic evolution example (see **Modes**).

If any two `Repr`s are identity equal, a non deterministic exception is thrown.
If there is a smallest index `j` such that `rj.mutate{i -> i.evaluate}` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `rj.mutate{i -> i.evaluate}` returns.\
Otherwise every `Repr` in `rs` is evaluated, and the flow behaves as `.any` over them (see **example with filter, under a short-circuit terminal**).

Without the `++ {}`, the `any` could decide before the `map` runs on every element, so running the `map` in parallel is positive speculation over mutations, and the whole flow expression throws the non deterministic exception of **example with filter, over ParRepr**.
With it, the `map` runs on every element in every strategy: a data parallel region with no positive speculation.
Unlike `.sort`, `++` also turns every element `read`: after it no stage can mutate a `Repr`, the fence ensures that nothing else is still mutating them, and the lambdas carry the `onlyImmCapture` flag, so they reach nothing else mutable. Positive speculation in the `any` terminal is then unobservable, as in `Par`.
After `.sort{..}` instead, the elements are still `mut Repr`s and the stages after the fence can not use positive speculation.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
IdentityCheck#rs;
for(mut Repr[Individual] r: rs){ r.mutate{i -> i.evaluate}; }
for(mut Repr[Individual] r: rs){
  if(r.look{i -> i.fitness > 90}){ return True; }
  }
return False;
```
The `map` returns its own element, so the list the fence materialises is `rs` itself.

Data parallel, with positive speculation after the fence:
```
Mailbox[Nat] done= fork(Range c: Chunks#(rs.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){ rs.get(j).mutate{i -> i.evaluate}; }
  };
IdentityCheck#rs;
done.join;
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(rs.size)){
  for(Nat j: c){
    if(!rs.get(j).look{i -> i.fitness > 90}){ continue; }
    out.send(j);
    break;
    }
  }.first.isSome;
```
`done.join` is the fence: the second data parallel region starts only once every `Repr` is evaluated.

**example with repeated elements: memoisation by identity**\
`orders.flow.map{o -> o.product}.map{p -> Price#p}.list`\
with `orders` an `imm List[imm Order]` where many orders share one `imm Product` object, and `Price#p` an expensive computation returning a `Nat`.

The elements arriving at the `map` stage calling `Price` repeat: many are identity equal. `Price#p` has no `mut` input, so by Determinism a call on an element identity equal to one already seen returns a structurally equivalent result. The result is `imm`, so by Identity Transparency reusing the cached result is unobservable: the second `map` can keep an `IdentityHashMap` from element to result, per task or shared between tasks.
A cached result may skip a non deterministic exception, like a stack overflow, that a recomputation would meet; non deterministic exceptions carry no such guarantee.

Both conditions are needed:
- no `mut` input: the element is `read` or `imm`. After a `flatMapMut`, one `mut` element may appear twice and be mutated by a stage between the two calls.
- an `imm` result: in `.map{p -> Persons#(p.name)}` the cache would give one `mut Person` to two elements, creating the duplicated `mut` elements of **Duplicated `mut` elements** where the sequential strategy creates two objects that are not identity equal.

The lookups pay off only when elements repeat, so this is a choice for a hint like those under **Chunk sizes**.

**Pseudocode:**

Sequential strategy:
```
IdentityMap[Product, Nat] cache= IdentityMap#;
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Order o: orders){
  Product p= o.product;
  Opt[Nat] hit= cache.get(p);
  if(hit.isSome){ res.add(hit!); continue; }
  Nat x= Price#p;
  cache.put(p, x);
  res.add(x);
  }
return res.list;
```
Data parallel, with one cache per task:
```
return fork(Range c: Chunks#(orders.size)){
  IdentityMap[Product, Nat] cache= IdentityMap#;
  for(Nat j: c){
    Product p= orders.get(j).product;
    Opt[Nat] hit= cache.get(p);
    if(hit.isSome){ out.send(hit!); continue; }
    Nat x= Price#p;
    cache.put(p, x);
    out.send(x);
    }
  }.list;
```
A call of `Price` that throws puts nothing in the cache, so the exception is thrown at the smallest index where the sequential strategy without the cache throws it.


## Extra interesting combinations of operators

Each entry below is a combination of operators with a rewrite that is legal, or that looks legal and is not.
A rewrite is legal when the whole flow expression returns the same for every lambda, including those that throw or do not terminate, and makes only calls that the under-specified choices allow, the same choices in every strategy.
The rewrites of **Canonical form** (see **example with limit**) are not repeated here.

### Ordering errors

An ordering error is a sequence of operations in which a stage is pointless: in that shape, what the stage computes is discarded or overridden by the operations after it, and removing or moving the stage changes the result only when some call throws or does not terminate. Such sequences are written by mistake, so the terminal throws an ordering error: a non deterministic exception naming the shape to write instead, as for the other misuses of flows (see **Modes**). The terminal sees the whole flow expression however it was built, so the check needs nothing from the compiler; a linter can report many of the same errors statically.
An ordering error is decided by the shape of the flow expression alone: the sequence of its operations, its mode and the types of its elements as far as the operations reveal them: the mode of the source, the `read` or `imm` elements that `++`, `flatMap`, `actor` and `scan` emit, and the number type of the argument of `.sum`; the chain records no other element type (see **Stage 1: Naive**). It never depends on the values of the arguments: not on whether two `by` or two `p` are the same object or structurally equivalent, not on whether the list passed to `++` is empty, not on the number passed to `limit`. It never looks into the lambdas: not at what a lambda computes, nor at whether it can throw or not terminate. It never depends on the algorithm implementing a stage either: which calls of `.cmp` and `.hash` a stage makes stays under-specified (see **How `.cmp` and `.hash` are called**). The optimiser is a separate matter: where the lambdas are simple enough, as `{::>3}` over a `Nat`, which can not throw, the backend may perform a rewrite that is not legal in general, but whether a sequence is an ordering error never depends on it: `.sort by .filter{::>3}.list` is an ordering error at every stage.
<HELP ME WITH WORDING> Example message: ``The flow expression sorts with `.sort by` and then drops elements with `.filter p`: the sort also orders the elements the filter drops. Write `.filter p .sort by`. To keep this order, call `.suppressOrderingErrors` on the flow.``

`mut .suppressOrderingErrors: mut Flow[E]` records the flag and emits every element it pulls, unchanged. It works like a hint: it can be called anywhere in the flow expression, conventionally at its beginning with the hints (see **Chunk sizes**), and it suppresses every ordering error of the whole flow expression. It can not say which error it is meant for: that imprecision is accepted, since asking for it at the exact place of each error would make the flow expressions hard to write and to rearrange.

The ordering errors, with the reason the stage is pointless and the shape the message suggests. 'Over `read` or `imm` elements' means that the shape shows that no element can lead to a `mut` object existing before the flow: mode `Par`, or a `++`, a `flatMap`, an `actor` or a `scan` before the stage. A `mut` object a stage creates after that point is reachable from nowhere else, so what the stages do to it is not observable outside the whole flow expression (see **Modes**).
- **Discarded order**: `.sort by` directly before `.size`, `.isEmpty`, `.get`, `.getOpt`, or `.sum` with a `Nat`, `Byte` or `Num` as its argument, also with only elementwise stages (`map`, `filter`, `flatMap`, `flatMapMut`, `mapFilter`, `peek`) between the sort and the terminal when those stages run over `read` or `imm` elements. None of these terminals reads the order: `.get` and `.getOpt` complete successfully only when at most one element arrives; the number types implement `SumNumber` as a fold with the checked `+`, where a sum of `Num` is exact and a sum of `Nat` or `Byte` overflows in one order exactly when it overflows in every order; and the elementwise stages do not read the order. With an `Int` or a `Float` the order decides whether the sum overflows and how it rounds, and any other `SumNumber` is user code that may read the order: no error. Over `mut` elements, as under `seqFlow`, a stage between the sort and the terminal may mutate its element, and the sort decides the order of those mutations: no error. Suggests removing the sort.
- **Unread results**: `.map f` or `.scan(acc, h)` directly before `.size` or `.isEmpty`, over `read` or `imm` elements. The calls of `f` and `h` can only throw or not terminate, and `.size` and `.isEmpty` read none of their results. Suggests removing the stage.
- **Two sorting stages**: `.sort by1 .sort by2`, whatever `by1` and `by2` are. The second sort reorders every element, and the order of the first survives only among the elements `by2` ranks together: the two stages are one sort with a composed order. The message offers both `.sort(by2.then by1)`, what the two stages compute, and `.sort(by1.then by2)`: two stable sorts give priority to the last one, while the user may mean `by1` as the primary order.
- **Two limits**: `.limit n .limit m`. Whatever `n` and `m` are, the limit with the greater number stops nothing that the other does not stop. Suggests a single `.limit(n.min(m))`.
- **Filter after sort**: `.sort by .filter p` over `read` or `imm` elements, with no decision point after the filter (see **Sort before a search** below). The sort also orders the elements the filter drops, and nothing reads their place; over `mut` elements `p` may mutate its element on purpose, in the sorted order, as for `.map f .size` below. Suggests `.filter p .sort by`. Where **Discarded order** also applies, as in `.sort by .filter p .size`, the message suggests removing the sort.

Close calls that are not ordering errors:
- **Sort before a search**: `.sort by` followed by a decision point, directly or through a `.filter p` and other elementwise stages: a short-circuit terminal (`.first`, `.findFirst p`, `.findMap f`, `.any p`, `.all p`, `.none p`), a `limit`, or an `actor` or `actorMut`, whose lambda may return `Break#`. The order decides which elements the search visits first, and so on which elements the lambdas after the sort are called, as when sorting by a cheap heuristic and then searching for the first element passing an expensive `p`. This holds for `.any`, `.all` and `.none` too, although they return the same `Bool` in every order. So `.sort by .findFirst p`, `.sort by .filter p .first` and `.sort by .any p` are not ordering errors.
- `.max by1 .max by2`, and any two `.max` or `.min` in a row: the second stage selects among the elements the first keeps.
- `.map f .map g`: each stage computes what the next one reads.
- `.filter p .filter q`: `p` is often a guard that `q` relies on, as in `.filter{x -> x != 0}.filter{x -> 12 / x > 2}`.
- `.sort by .first`, `.sort by .last`, `.sort by .limit k`: the order of the elements kept is read.
- `.sort by .set(..)`, `.sort by .eSet(..)`, `.sort by .mapping(..)`: the order decides which element of each class a `Set` keeps, and the order of the elements of an `ESet` and of the entries of the map.
- `.sort by1 .sortDistinct by2`, `.sort by1 .distinct by2` and `.sortDistinct by1 .sortDistinct by2`: the order of the first stage decides which element of each class the second keeps, as when sorting by fitness and keeping the fittest of each species.
- `.sortDistinct by1 .sort by2`: the first stage also drops the repeated elements, which the second does not; only an `OrderHashBy` would allow `.distinct by1 .sort(by2.then by1)`, and the shape does not see which `by1` is.
- `.distinct by .filter p` and `.sortDistinct by .filter p`: when two elements of one class disagree on `p`, the two orders keep different elements; when `p` rejects the element of a class with the smallest index and accepts one after it, only `.filter p .distinct by` keeps that one.
- `.map f .limit n`: it makes the same calls as `.limit n .map f` (see **Canonical form**), and neither stage is pointless.
- `.map f .last`: the last result of `f` is read.
- `.map f .size` over `mut` elements, as in `persons.seqFlow.map{p -> Block#(p.birthday, p)}.size`: `f` may mutate its `mut` element on purpose.
- `.peek f .size`, in every mode: the calls of `f` are the purpose of `peek`, checking invariants or printing with `Debug#`, so `peek` is a step of its own in the shape, not the `map` it desugars to.
- `.map(ctx, g) .size` and `.peek(ctx, f) .size`, in every mode: the context of map with context can carry IO.
- `.distinct by1 .distinct by2`, `.max by1 .sort by2`, and the other sequences whose second stage is pointless only when the two arguments are equal: the arguments are not part of the shape.

### The combinations

**scan then limit**\
`l.flow.scan(0, h).limit 3 .list`\
with `h` of type `read F[Nat,Nat,Nat]`.

Let `s0` be `h#(0, e0)` and `sj` be `h#(s(j-1), ej)`. If there is a smallest index `j` among `0`, `1` and `2` such that computing `sj` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what that call returns. Otherwise it returns `Lists#(s0, s1, s2)`.

Legal: `.scan(acc, h) .limit n` is `.limit n .scan(acc, h)`, so the limit moves on to the source. `scan` pushes exactly one element for each element it pulls and never returns `Break#`: it goes 1-1 as `map` does, and the element at index `3` is never pulled in either order. The same holds for map with context, and for any `actor` or `actorMut` whose inlined lambda pushes exactly once and returns `Continue#` on every path. An actor that may push no element or two, as the window of **Example for actor**, can not be crossed.

Sequential strategy:
```
Nat s= 0;
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat e: l.take(3)){
  s= h#(s, e);
  res.add(s);
  }
return res.list;
```
Pipeline parallelism:
```
Nat s= 0;
return node(l.take(3)){e, out -> s= h#(s, e); out.send(s); }.list;
```

**scan then last**\
`l.flow.scan(0, h).last`

If `l` is empty, the whole flow expression returns `Opt#`. Otherwise, with `sj` as above, if there is a smallest index `j` such that computing `sj` throws or does not terminate, it returns what that call returns; otherwise it returns `Opts#s7`.

Legal: the calls of `h` are those of a fold, in the same order; only the last accumulator is kept, and nothing is materialised.
```
Nat s= 0;
for(Nat e: l){ s= h#(s, e); }
if(l.isEmpty){ return Opt#; }
return Opts#s;
```
`.scan(acc, h).last` gives no parallelism beyond that of a fold: the calls of `h` form a sequence (see **actor, actorMut, scan**).

**max then limit, sort then last**\
`l.flow.map f .max by .limit 2 .list` and `l.flow.map f .sort by .last`\
with `by` an `OrderBy[Nat]`.

For `.max by .limit 2`: if there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns. Otherwise, if some calls of `by` made by the max tree throw or do not terminate, it returns what the one the tree makes before the others returns. Otherwise it returns the first two, in flow order, of the elements tied for the maximum.

Legal: a merge of the max tree compares only the front elements of its two blocks, so capping every block at `2` elements changes no call; it only stops carrying long runs of ties.
```
List[Nat] maxMerge(List[Nat] a, List[Nat] b, OrderBy[Nat] by, Nat k){
  Ord o= by.cmp(a.get(0), b.get(0));
  if(o.isGt){ return a; }
  if(o.isLt){ return b; }
  return (a ++ b).take(k);
  }
```
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return Max#(vs, by, 2);
```
where `Max#(vs, by, k)` is the tree of `Max#` with this merge.

`.sort by .last` is the greatest element under `by`, the one with the greatest index among ties, since the sort is stable. A merge tree keeping only the last element of each block makes one call per merge:
```
List[Nat] lastMerge(List[Nat] a, List[Nat] b, OrderBy[Nat] by){
  if(by.cmp(a.get(0), b.get(0)).isGt){ return a; }
  return b;
  }
```
Legal as a choice of sorting algorithm, as the truncated merges of **example with sort and limit**: it makes different calls than `Sort#`, and for a `by` not respecting its contract it may return a different element, so every strategy must make this choice. The data parallel version is `MergeTree#` with this merge.

**distinct then first**\
`l.flow.map f .distinct by .first`\
with `by` an `OrderHashBy[Nat]`.

If `l` is empty, the whole flow expression returns `Opt#`. Otherwise, if `f#e0` throws or does not terminate, it returns what `f#e0` returns. Otherwise the distinct stage keeps `f#e0`, since no value was kept before it, and `.first` stops the flow: it returns `Opts#(f#e0)`.

Legal: which calls of `by` the distinct stage makes is under-specified. The first element is kept whatever `by` answers, and its hash is needed only to find it again for the elements after it, which `.first` never pulls: the choice of making no call on it serves every strategy. `f` runs only on `e0`, since `.first` breaks right after it.
```
for(Nat e: l){ return Opts#(f#e); }
return Opt#;
```
With `.limit 2` in place of `.first`, the second element has to be compared with the first, so `by` is called.

**sort then filter**\
`l.flow.map f .sort by .filter p .list`\
with `by` an `OrderBy[Nat]` and `p` a `read F[Nat,Bool]`.

Ordering error (see **Ordering errors**); the results and the first pseudocode below are what runs under `.suppressOrderingErrors`.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `f#ej` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression returns what `f#ej` returns. Otherwise, if some calls of `by` made by the sorting process throw or do not terminate, it returns what the one the sorting process makes before the others returns. Otherwise let `v0,...,v7` be the `f#ej` in `by` order, ties ordered by smallest index: if there is a smallest `k` such that `p#vk` throws or does not terminate, it returns what `p#vk` returns; otherwise it returns, in that order, the `vk` for which `p#vk` completes successfully with `True`.
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat v: Sort#(vs, by, vs.size)){
  if(!p#v){ continue; }
  res.add(v);
  }
return res.list;
```
Looks legal, is not in general: `.filter p .sort by` sorts fewer elements, and the calls of `by` are under-specified, but the calls of `p` move:
- they run before the calls of `by`, so an exception of `p` would come before one of `by`: this alone could be fixed by holding it;
- the flow order after the sort decides which exception of `p` propagates, so an element whose `p` call throws would still have to be sorted;
- a call of `p` that does not terminate stops the sequential strategy at the smallest index, while the result is decided by the smallest `k` in `by` order: if `p` does not terminate on `f#e0` and throws on an element sorted before `f#e0`, the sequential strategy of the rewrite never returns, while the whole flow expression throws. A parallel strategy could run the calls of `p` as tasks and wait only for those it needs, but the sequential strategy can not, and the choice of which elements are sorted must be the same in every strategy.

So, under `.suppressOrderingErrors`, the rewrite is legal only when `p` can neither throw nor fail to terminate, which the backend knows only from inlined code such as `{::>3}` over a `Nat`; then the backend performs it:
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){
  Nat v= f#e;
  if(v > 3){ vs.add(v); }
  }
return Sort#(vs, by, vs.size);
```
The pseudocode of `.filter p .sort by` is the loop above with `p#v` in place of `v > 3`.
Under `.suppressOrderingErrors`, running `p` early without changing the calls of `by` is still possible, as in **example with elementwise work after a fence**.

**sort then sort**\
`l.flow.map f .sort by1 .sort by2 .list`\
with `by1` and `by2` of type `OrderBy[Nat]`.

The second sort receives the output of the first, and each makes its own calls; the result is the output of the second sort, or what the first call that throws or does not terminate returns, in the order of the semantics of **example with a fence**.
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return Sort#(Sort#(vs, by1, vs.size), by2, vs.size);
```
Ordering error (see **Ordering errors**); the result and the pseudocode above are what runs under `.suppressOrderingErrors`.
Looks legal, is not: the second sort is stable, so for comparators respecting their contract the result is ordered by `by2`, with ties ordered by `by1`, as one `.sort(by2.then by1)`; but the calls of `by1` differ, and a comparator not respecting its contract gives a different order.
The pseudocode of `.sort(by2.then by1)`:
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return Sort#(vs, by2.then by1, vs.size);
```

**a stage whose result nothing reads**\
`l.flow.map f .sort by .sort by .list`, `l.flow.map f .sort by .size`, `l.flow.map f .last`, and `l.flow.map f .size` in mode `Par`\
with `by` an `OrderBy[Nat]`.

`.sort by .sort by`, `.sort by .size` and `.map f .size` in mode `Par` are ordering errors, `.map f .last` is not (see **Ordering errors**); the pseudocode below is what runs under `.suppressOrderingErrors`.

In each, a stage runs although, for well behaved lambdas, the result would be the same without it: the second sort receives a list already sorted by `by`, `.size` depends neither on the order nor on the results of `f`, and `.last` reads only the last result of `f`.
None of those stages can be dropped: its calls may throw or not terminate, and `by` is user code that nothing forces to be consistent between the two sorts (see **Warning** in **example with distinct, on a repeated element**). This is the reasoning of **example with size**.
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return Sort#(Sort#(vs, by, vs.size), by, vs.size);
```
```
EList[Nat] vs= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){ vs.add(f#e); }
return Sort#(vs, by, vs.size).size;
```
```
Opt[Nat] res= Opt#;
for(Nat e: l){ res= Opts#(f#e); }
return res;
```
What is legal is a cheaper choice of algorithm: a sort that first compares the adjacent pairs of its input makes only `7` calls on an already sorted input, and every strategy can make those calls.

**flatMap then flatMap**\
`l.flow.flatMap f .flatMap g .limit 3 .list`\
with `f` and `g` of type `read F[Nat, read List[Nat]]`.

The whole flow expression pulls the elements of `l` in flow order; for each element `e`, it computes `f#e` and, for each element `a` of it in order, `g#a`, emitting its elements. It stops right after the call of `g` whose list holds the third element emitted: the first call that throws or does not terminate before that decides what it returns; otherwise it returns the first three elements emitted.
```
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){
  for(Nat a: f#e){
    for(Nat x: g#a){
      res.add(x);
      if(res.size == 3){ return res.list; }
      }
    }
  }
return res.list;
```
Looks legal, is not: `.flatMap f .flatMap g` looks associative, as `.flatMap{x -> f#x .flow.flatMap g .list}`, but the lambda of `.flatMap` builds its whole list before emitting any element of it (see **Fenced expansions**). The candidate calls `g` on every element of `f#e0`, even after the third element is emitted.
With `f#0` returning `Lists#(1, 2)`, `g#1` returning `Lists#(10, 11, 12)` and `g#2` throwing, the whole flow expression returns `Lists#(10, 11, 12)`, and the candidate throws.
With a consuming terminal and no decision point after the two stages, the calls and their order are the same, and the rewrite is legal.

**a fence before a short-circuit terminal**\
`ps.seqFlow.map{p -> Block#(p.birthday, p)} ++ {} .first`\
with `ps` a `mut List[mut Person]`.

If `ps` is empty, the whole flow expression returns `Opt#`. Otherwise, if there is a smallest index `j` such that `pj.birthday` throws or does not terminate, it returns what that call returns. Otherwise every person in `ps` is one year older, and it returns `Opts#p0`, as `read`.

Looks like a limit that can move towards the source, and is not: `.first` is `.limit 1 .getOpt`, but `++` is a fence, and every element goes through the stages before it before `++` emits any element. A limit never moves before a fence, and the `++ {}` written to start a data parallel region also runs the stages before it on every element.
```
for(mut Person p: ps){ p.birthday; }
for(mut Person p: ps){ return Opts#p; }
return Opt#;
```
The `map` returns its own element, so the list the fence materialises is `ps` itself.

**recomputing instead of storing**\
`l.flow.map f .filter p .list`\
with `p` a `read F[Nat,Bool]`.

The whole flow expression returns what the sequential strategy returns: `f` and then `p` on each element, in flow order.

Legal but not a rewrite to perform: `.map f .filter p` returns the same as `.filter{x -> p#(f#x)}.map f`. On an element that passes, the candidate calls `f` twice with the same `imm` input: by Determinism the second call returns a structurally equivalent result, and it can not throw or fail to terminate since the first completed successfully. With `mut` elements, as under `seqFlow`, `f` may mutate its element, and the second call is observable.
This costs a call and gains nothing here, but the same reasoning lets a backend recompute a result instead of storing it, as the slots of **example with elementwise work after a fence** do:
```
EList[Nat] res= EList#;
for(Nat e: l){
  if(!p#(f#e)){ continue; }
  res.add(f#e);
  }
return res.list;
```

